1
00:00:58,440 --> 00:01:01,070
Gentlemen, cock your pistols.

2
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:06,530
Gentlemen!

3
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:11,660
Barry's father had been bred, like many sons of genteel families...

4
00:01:12,370 --> 00:01:14,330
...to the profession of the law.

5
00:01:14,620 --> 00:01:18,580
There is no doubt he would've made an eminent figure in his profession...

6
00:01:19,210 --> 00:01:21,460
...had he not been killed in a duel...

7
00:01:22,250 --> 00:01:24,880
...which arose over the purchase of some horses.

8
00:01:39,940 --> 00:01:43,070
Barry's mother, after her husband's death...

9
00:01:43,650 --> 00:01:46,280
...lived in such a way as to defy slander.

10
00:01:47,990 --> 00:01:51,620
Many a man who had been charmed by the spinster...

11
00:01:51,990 --> 00:01:53,950
...renewed his offers to the widow.

12
00:01:55,490 --> 00:01:58,120
But she refused all proposals of marriage...

13
00:01:58,870 --> 00:02:01,790
...declaring that she lived only for her son...

14
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:04,800
...and the memory of her departed saint.

15
00:02:56,810 --> 00:03:01,810
First love! What a change it makes in a lad.

16
00:03:03,190 --> 00:03:06,480
What a magnificent secret it is he carries with him!

17
00:03:08,820 --> 00:03:12,570
The tender passion gushes out of a man's heart.

18
00:03:13,780 --> 00:03:16,410
He loves as a bird sings...

19
00:03:16,950 --> 00:03:19,660
...or a rose blows from nature.

20
00:03:22,250 --> 00:03:23,330
Killarney.

21
00:03:26,040 --> 00:03:28,340
Now, what shall it be?

22
00:03:32,510 --> 00:03:34,470
Turn around and face the wall.

23
00:03:44,520 --> 00:03:46,810
The object of Barry's attention...

24
00:03:46,980 --> 00:03:49,570
...and the cause of his early troubles...

25
00:03:49,730 --> 00:03:53,150
...was his cousin, Nora Brady by name.

26
00:04:05,290 --> 00:04:09,420
I have hidden my neck ribbon somewhere on my person.

27
00:04:11,210 --> 00:04:13,340
If you find it, you can have it.

28
00:04:14,510 --> 00:04:16,760
You are free to look anywhere for it.

29
00:04:17,550 --> 00:04:20,350
I will think little of you if you do not find it.

30
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:49,420
I cannot find it.

31
00:04:52,050 --> 00:04:53,880
You haven't looked properly.

32
00:05:07,890 --> 00:05:09,230
I cannot find it.

33
00:05:15,780 --> 00:05:17,280
I'll give you a hint.

34
00:05:36,920 --> 00:05:38,720
I feel the ribbon.

35
00:05:43,970 --> 00:05:45,430
Why are you trembling?

36
00:05:51,060 --> 00:05:53,400
At the pleasure of finding the ribbon.

37
00:06:00,570 --> 00:06:01,570
Liar.

38
00:06:18,300 --> 00:06:23,180
Company, forward march!

39
00:07:01,130 --> 00:07:04,470
Company, eyes left!

40
00:07:07,430 --> 00:07:11,890
About this time the United Kingdom was greatly excited...

41
00:07:12,350 --> 00:07:14,310
...by the threat...

42
00:07:14,480 --> 00:07:16,270
...of a French invasion.

43
00:07:17,520 --> 00:07:19,900
The noblemen and people of condition...

44
00:07:20,070 --> 00:07:22,150
...loyally raised regiments...

45
00:07:23,450 --> 00:07:25,200
...to resist the invaders.

46
00:07:26,950 --> 00:07:31,080
Their scarlet coats and swaggering airs filled Barry with envy.

47
00:07:37,670 --> 00:07:41,340
Company, eyes right!

48
00:07:47,470 --> 00:07:49,510
Brady Town sent a company to join...

49
00:07:49,680 --> 00:07:53,770
...the Kilwangan Regiment, of which John Quin was the Captain.

50
00:07:56,940 --> 00:07:59,610
The whole country was alive with war's alarms...

51
00:08:00,610 --> 00:08:03,780
...the kingdoms ringing with military music.

52
00:08:10,040 --> 00:08:11,500
At ready!

53
00:08:15,210 --> 00:08:16,670
Present!

54
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:20,500
Fire!

55
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:47,420
Redmond, what is the matter?

56
00:09:52,350 --> 00:09:55,930
Were you obliged to dance five times with Captain Quin?

57
00:09:57,060 --> 00:09:59,520
I don't care a fig for Captain Quin.

58
00:10:01,020 --> 00:10:04,110
He dances prettily, and is a pleasant rattle of a man.

59
00:10:04,360 --> 00:10:06,480
He looks well in his regimentals.

60
00:10:06,690 --> 00:10:09,280
He asked me to dance. How could I refuse him?

61
00:10:09,530 --> 00:10:10,990
But you refused me.

62
00:10:11,280 --> 00:10:13,240
I can dance with you any day.

63
00:10:14,240 --> 00:10:18,250
To dance with my cousin looks as though I could find no other partner.

64
00:10:19,620 --> 00:10:22,920
Besides, Captain Quin is a man.

65
00:10:24,040 --> 00:10:26,800
You're only a boy and penniless.

66
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:31,430
If I meet him again, you will find out who is the best man.

67
00:10:31,800 --> 00:10:34,260
I'll fight him Captain as he is.

68
00:10:34,510 --> 00:10:36,060
Oh, don't be so silly!

69
00:10:36,220 --> 00:10:37,520
I mean it.

70
00:10:38,060 --> 00:10:41,310
But Captain Quin is known as a valiant soldier.

71
00:10:42,060 --> 00:10:44,810
It is mighty well of you to fight yokels...

72
00:10:45,070 --> 00:10:47,820
...but to fight an Englishman is a different matter.

73
00:10:49,320 --> 00:10:51,320
You best have him take you home.

74
00:10:52,160 --> 00:10:53,240
Redmond!

75
00:11:20,480 --> 00:11:23,100
Barry resolved never to see Nora again.

76
00:11:24,310 --> 00:11:28,190
But such resolutions, steadfastly held for a whole week...

77
00:11:29,030 --> 00:11:31,650
...are abandoned in a few moments of despair.

78
00:11:35,450 --> 00:11:40,040
No, Nora, no, except for you and four others...

79
00:11:40,370 --> 00:11:44,000
...I vow before all the gods my heart...

80
00:11:44,870 --> 00:11:46,960
...has never felt the soft flame.

81
00:11:48,130 --> 00:11:51,880
Ah, you men, you men, John...

82
00:11:52,880 --> 00:11:55,180
...your passion's not equal to ours.

83
00:11:56,550 --> 00:11:59,060
We are like some plant I've read of.

84
00:12:00,100 --> 00:12:03,060
We bear but one flower and then we die.

85
00:12:04,440 --> 00:12:07,060
You, you, you mean you...

86
00:12:07,270 --> 00:12:09,860
...never felt an inclination for another?

87
00:12:10,030 --> 00:12:13,740
Never, my John. How can you ask such a question?

88
00:12:13,900 --> 00:12:15,240
Oh, darling Norelia!

89
00:12:18,070 --> 00:12:21,870
Nora was chaperoned by her brothers Mick and Ulick...

90
00:12:22,910 --> 00:12:25,250
...whose interests would be affected...

91
00:12:25,420 --> 00:12:28,710
...by the favorable outcome of her affair with Quin.

92
00:12:41,560 --> 00:12:43,680
Redmond, how nice to see you.

93
00:12:48,400 --> 00:12:49,400
Redmond!

94
00:12:50,980 --> 00:12:52,780
How could you do this to me?

95
00:12:53,530 --> 00:12:55,490
Redmond, what's the matter?

96
00:12:55,650 --> 00:12:56,950
What are you saying?

97
00:12:59,570 --> 00:13:03,410
I think this might be the moment to return something to you.

98
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:06,790
I--

99
00:13:07,750 --> 00:13:09,210
Thank you, Redmond.

100
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:11,920
I must have forgotten it.

101
00:13:13,170 --> 00:13:14,260
Yes, you did.

102
00:13:19,090 --> 00:13:22,760
Captain Quin, may I introduce my cousin, Redmond Barry.

103
00:13:23,100 --> 00:13:25,310
Miss Brady, it would appear...

104
00:13:25,480 --> 00:13:28,020
...you both have something private to discuss.

105
00:13:28,190 --> 00:13:30,480
It would be best for me to withdraw.

106
00:13:30,940 --> 00:13:34,070
Captain Quin, I have nothing private to discuss with my cousin.

107
00:13:34,860 --> 00:13:38,110
Miss Brady, it appears you have a great deal to discuss.

108
00:13:39,110 --> 00:13:43,410
Captain Quin, he don't signify any more than my parrot or lapdog.

109
00:13:43,700 --> 00:13:44,790
Indeed!

110
00:13:44,950 --> 00:13:46,960
Are you in the habit of giving...

111
00:13:47,290 --> 00:13:50,880
...intimate articles of your clothing to your parrot or lapdog?

112
00:13:51,630 --> 00:13:54,250
Mayn't I give a bit of ribbon to my own cousin?

113
00:13:55,210 --> 00:13:56,710
You're welcome, miss.

114
00:13:57,470 --> 00:13:59,180
As many yards as you like.

115
00:14:01,090 --> 00:14:04,970
When ladies make presents to gentlemen, it's time for other gentlemen to retire.

116
00:14:05,140 --> 00:14:07,180
I wish you both a good day.

117
00:14:07,390 --> 00:14:08,430
Jack Quin!

118
00:14:09,140 --> 00:14:10,480
What's the matter?

119
00:14:10,650 --> 00:14:12,230
I'll tell you, sir.

120
00:14:12,400 --> 00:14:15,020
I've had enough of Miss Brady and your Irish ways.

121
00:14:15,190 --> 00:14:16,230
Ain't used to them.

122
00:14:16,400 --> 00:14:17,400
What is it?

123
00:14:17,570 --> 00:14:21,110
We'll accustom you to Irish ways, or we'll adopt English ones.

124
00:14:21,450 --> 00:14:24,620
It is not the English way for ladies to have two lovers.

125
00:14:25,200 --> 00:14:28,160
And so, I'll thank you to pay me the sum you owe me.

126
00:14:28,500 --> 00:14:31,420
And I resign all claims to this young lady.

127
00:14:31,830 --> 00:14:34,290
If she fancies schoolboys, let her take 'em, sir.

128
00:14:34,460 --> 00:14:35,710
You're joking!

129
00:14:36,420 --> 00:14:38,050
Ha. I never was more earnest.

130
00:14:40,300 --> 00:14:41,590
John, wait.

131
00:14:47,470 --> 00:14:49,140
Meddling brat!

132
00:14:49,890 --> 00:14:51,900
Your hand is in everybody's pie!

133
00:14:52,150 --> 00:14:56,860
What business had you to quarrel with a gentleman of substance?

134
00:15:51,750 --> 00:15:54,000
Redmond, me boy, take a seat.

135
00:17:07,240 --> 00:17:10,240
Mrs. Brady and ladies, if you please.

136
00:17:12,160 --> 00:17:16,710
This sort of toast is drunk too seldom in my family...

137
00:17:17,040 --> 00:17:20,330
...and you'll please to receive it with all honors.

138
00:17:20,590 --> 00:17:24,710
Here's to Captain and Mrs. John Quin and long life to them.

139
00:17:29,930 --> 00:17:30,930
Go on.

140
00:17:31,510 --> 00:17:34,470
Kiss her, Jack, for faith you've got a treasure.

141
00:17:36,480 --> 00:17:37,770
Come on, Jack.

142
00:17:39,600 --> 00:17:40,980
There's the man!

143
00:17:49,910 --> 00:17:52,200
A long and happy life together.

144
00:17:52,620 --> 00:17:54,740
A long and happy life together.

145
00:17:55,410 --> 00:17:56,700
Thank you.

146
00:18:01,420 --> 00:18:02,420
Redmond!

147
00:18:13,550 --> 00:18:16,850
Here's my toast to you, Captain John Quin.

148
00:18:19,980 --> 00:18:23,610
Redmond, how dare you behave like that in my house!

149
00:18:24,320 --> 00:18:26,400
Mrs. Brady, take the children out.

150
00:18:26,570 --> 00:18:29,240
My dear fellow, are you all right?

151
00:18:38,580 --> 00:18:41,920
In heaven's name, what does all the row mean?

152
00:18:42,960 --> 00:18:44,210
The fact is, sir...

153
00:18:44,380 --> 00:18:46,300
...the young monkey's in love with Nora.

154
00:18:46,460 --> 00:18:49,510
He found her and the Captain in the garden today.

155
00:18:49,670 --> 00:18:51,760
Now he's for murdering Jack Quin.

156
00:18:52,590 --> 00:18:56,220
Mr. Brady, I've been insulted grossly in this house.

157
00:18:56,930 --> 00:18:59,600
I'm not satisfied with these ways of going on.

158
00:18:59,890 --> 00:19:02,690
I'm an Englishman! And a man of property!

159
00:19:03,900 --> 00:19:06,190
And this impudent young swine...

160
00:19:06,650 --> 00:19:07,860
...should be horsewhipped!

161
00:19:08,030 --> 00:19:10,740
Mr. Quin can have satisfaction any time he pleases...

162
00:19:10,900 --> 00:19:13,990
...by calling on Redmond Barry, Esquire of Barryville.

163
00:19:21,910 --> 00:19:23,460
I'll see the boy home.

164
00:19:31,630 --> 00:19:34,840
A pretty day's work, Master Redmond.

165
00:19:36,050 --> 00:19:38,220
Knowing your uncle is distressed for money...

166
00:19:38,390 --> 00:19:42,690
will bring $1,500 a year into the family.......trying to break off a match which

167
00:19:43,480 --> 00:19:48,110
 Quin has promised to pay off the $4,000 which is bothering your uncle.

168
00:19:49,190 --> 00:19:51,280
He takes a girl without a penny...

169
00:19:51,440 --> 00:19:55,950
...a girl that's flinging herself at the head of every man in these parts.

170
00:19:56,620 --> 00:19:58,120
And missing them all!

171
00:19:59,330 --> 00:20:03,410
And you, who ought to be attached to your uncle as to your father....

172
00:20:03,830 --> 00:20:04,870
I am.

173
00:20:05,710 --> 00:20:08,500
And this is the return you make for his kindness?

174
00:20:08,790 --> 00:20:11,420
Didn't he harbor you when your father died?

175
00:20:12,470 --> 00:20:16,760
Hasn't he given you and your mother a fine house, rent free?

176
00:20:16,930 --> 00:20:19,310
Mark this, and come what will of it...

177
00:20:20,310 --> 00:20:23,600
...I will fight the man who pretends the hand of Nora Brady.

178
00:20:24,310 --> 00:20:27,860
I'll follow him if it's into the church and fight him there.

179
00:20:29,150 --> 00:20:31,610
I'll have his blood, or he'll have mine.

180
00:20:36,570 --> 00:20:38,280
Faith, and I believe ye!

181
00:20:39,240 --> 00:20:41,870
I never saw a lad more game in me life.

182
00:20:43,290 --> 00:20:44,870
Give me a kiss, me boy.

183
00:20:48,250 --> 00:20:49,960
You're after me own soul.

184
00:20:50,630 --> 00:20:55,170
As long as I live, you shall never want a friend or a second.

185
00:20:57,550 --> 00:21:00,850
Will you take my message to him? And arrange a meeting?

186
00:21:03,680 --> 00:21:06,980
Well, if it must be, it must.

187
00:22:13,960 --> 00:22:17,630
Look here, Redmond, me boy, this is a silly business.

188
00:22:18,760 --> 00:22:21,390
The girl will marry Quin, mark my words.

189
00:22:22,510 --> 00:22:25,310
And as sure as she does, you'll forget her.

190
00:22:26,180 --> 00:22:30,140
You're but a boy and Quin is willing to consider you as such.

191
00:22:31,940 --> 00:22:33,060
Right, Quin?

192
00:22:34,770 --> 00:22:35,780
Oh, mm-hm.

193
00:22:39,450 --> 00:22:41,530
Now, Dublin's a fine place.

194
00:22:42,660 --> 00:22:46,620
If you've a mind to ride there and see the town for a month...

195
00:22:46,990 --> 00:22:49,620
...here's ten guineas at your service.

196
00:22:51,670 --> 00:22:53,830
Will that satisfy you, Captain Quin?

197
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:58,340
Yes, if Mr. Barry will apologize and go to Dublin...

198
00:22:58,920 --> 00:23:02,220
...I will consider the whole affair honorably settled.

199
00:23:08,810 --> 00:23:10,520
Say you're sorry, Redmond.

200
00:23:12,440 --> 00:23:14,360
You can easily say that.

201
00:23:22,110 --> 00:23:23,360
I'm not sorry.

202
00:23:26,200 --> 00:23:27,870
And I'll not apologize.

203
00:23:31,080 --> 00:23:33,710
And I'd as soon go to Dublin as to hell.

204
00:23:42,090 --> 00:23:45,220
Then there's nothing else for it.

205
00:23:47,560 --> 00:23:48,890
God bless you.

206
00:23:53,640 --> 00:23:55,150
This isn't my pistol.

207
00:23:56,110 --> 00:23:58,110
It's all right, it's one of mine.

208
00:23:58,900 --> 00:24:01,820
Yours will serve, if it's needed, for the next round.

209
00:24:04,990 --> 00:24:06,450
Good luck, Redmond.

210
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:20,300
Gentlemen...

211
00:24:23,510 --> 00:24:25,010
...cock your pistols.

212
00:24:38,810 --> 00:24:40,070
Gentlemen...

213
00:24:42,400 --> 00:24:43,860
...aim your pistols.

214
00:24:57,960 --> 00:24:58,960
One.

215
00:25:01,590 --> 00:25:02,590
Two.

216
00:25:05,260 --> 00:25:06,340
Three.

217
00:25:16,770 --> 00:25:17,940
Is he dead?

218
00:25:22,400 --> 00:25:23,530
Quite dead.

219
00:25:29,620 --> 00:25:32,580
This has been a sad day's work for our family.

220
00:25:33,410 --> 00:25:35,700
Ye've robbed us of 1,500 a year.

221
00:25:36,620 --> 00:25:39,170
Now you'd better ride off before the Police are up.

222
00:25:39,330 --> 00:25:41,920
They'd wind of this before we left Kilwangan.

223
00:25:43,250 --> 00:25:45,710
Come on, Redmond, I'll go home with you.

224
00:26:08,990 --> 00:26:11,570
How different Barry's fate might have been...

225
00:26:12,200 --> 00:26:14,580
...had he not fallen in love with Nora...

226
00:26:14,950 --> 00:26:18,210
...and had he not flung the wine in Captain Quin's face.

227
00:26:19,080 --> 00:26:21,380
But he was destined to be a wanderer.

228
00:26:22,290 --> 00:26:26,210
And the battle with Quin set him on his travels at an early age...

229
00:26:27,380 --> 00:26:29,090
...as you shall soon see.

230
00:26:29,340 --> 00:26:32,720
The boy must go into hiding for a short time, anyway.

231
00:26:34,430 --> 00:26:36,140
Dublin's the best place for him.

232
00:26:36,310 --> 00:26:39,060
He can stay there 'til matters have blown over.

233
00:26:39,440 --> 00:26:42,690
But the child has never been away from home before.

234
00:26:43,610 --> 00:26:45,730
Wouldn't he be as safe here?

235
00:26:45,940 --> 00:26:48,780
I wish that were true, Aunt Belle.

236
00:26:49,110 --> 00:26:52,450
But the Bailiffs may be on their way already.

237
00:26:53,780 --> 00:26:56,950
Now, Dublin is five days' ride away from here.

238
00:26:57,200 --> 00:26:59,660
There's not a soul who'll know him there.

239
00:26:59,960 --> 00:27:02,960
I don't want to harp on unpleasant matters...

240
00:27:03,380 --> 00:27:07,420
...but you do know what can happen to him if he's taken.

241
00:27:16,600 --> 00:27:17,970
I'll be all right.

242
00:27:24,730 --> 00:27:26,940
I'll be all right in Dublin, Mother.

243
00:27:58,430 --> 00:28:03,060
No lad with liberty for the first time... ...and 20 guineas in his pocket...

244
00:28:03,560 --> 00:28:04,850
...is very sad.

245
00:28:05,650 --> 00:28:08,980
Barry rode towards Dublin thinking not so much of...

246
00:28:09,150 --> 00:28:12,440
...the kind mother left alone, and the home behind him...

247
00:28:13,320 --> 00:28:16,620
...but of tomorrow and all the wonders it would bring.

248
00:28:30,090 --> 00:28:31,420
Excuse me, miss.

249
00:28:31,840 --> 00:28:35,260
Would it be possible to have a drink of water? Yes, sir.

250
00:28:40,350 --> 00:28:42,100
Good day to you, young sir.

251
00:28:45,140 --> 00:28:46,190
Good day.

252
00:28:53,030 --> 00:28:54,860
Will you join us in a drink?

253
00:28:57,870 --> 00:28:59,370
No, thank you.

254
00:29:03,040 --> 00:29:05,040
Would you like something to eat?

255
00:29:07,580 --> 00:29:10,500
That's very kind of you, but I have to be on my way.

256
00:29:17,090 --> 00:29:18,180
Thank you.

257
00:29:31,150 --> 00:29:32,190
Goodbye.

258
00:30:21,910 --> 00:30:23,330
Uh, excuse me, sir.

259
00:30:25,790 --> 00:30:27,750
Good morning again, young sir.

260
00:30:32,210 --> 00:30:34,130
Don't even think about it.

261
00:30:36,920 --> 00:30:38,720
Get down off that horse.

262
00:30:45,930 --> 00:30:48,390
Raise your hands above your head, please.

263
00:30:49,730 --> 00:30:50,980
Come forward.

264
00:30:55,570 --> 00:30:56,570
Stop.

265
00:31:00,990 --> 00:31:04,280
How do you do? I'm Captain Feeney.

266
00:31:07,620 --> 00:31:08,910
Captain Feeney!

267
00:31:09,160 --> 00:31:11,620
Captain Feeney, at your service.

268
00:31:12,670 --> 00:31:14,130
The Captain Feeney?

269
00:31:15,000 --> 00:31:16,130
None other.

270
00:31:16,750 --> 00:31:19,340
May I introduce you to my son...Seamus?

271
00:31:22,010 --> 00:31:23,300
How do you do?

272
00:31:23,470 --> 00:31:24,600
How do you do?

273
00:31:25,260 --> 00:31:27,760
To whom have I the honor of speaking?

274
00:31:28,640 --> 00:31:30,310
My name's Redmond Barry.

275
00:31:30,480 --> 00:31:32,190
How do you do, Mr. Barry?

276
00:31:33,020 --> 00:31:37,480
Now we must get onto the more regrettable stage of our brief acquaintance.

277
00:31:37,690 --> 00:31:41,820
Turn around and keep your hands high above your head, please.

278
00:32:16,270 --> 00:32:18,900
There must be 20 guineas in gold here, Father.

279
00:32:19,690 --> 00:32:24,150
You seem to be a very well set up young gentleman, sir.

280
00:32:25,530 --> 00:32:29,120
Captain Feeney, that's all the money my mother had in the world.

281
00:32:29,280 --> 00:32:30,870
Mightn't I keep it?

282
00:32:31,200 --> 00:32:33,660
I'm just one step ahead of the Law myself.

283
00:32:33,870 --> 00:32:38,290
I killed an English Officer in a duel, and I'm going to Dublin until things cool down.

284
00:32:38,460 --> 00:32:39,460
Mr. Barry...

285
00:32:40,050 --> 00:32:43,010
...in my profession we hear many such stories.

286
00:32:43,300 --> 00:32:47,090
Yours is the most intriguing and touching I've heard in weeks.

287
00:32:47,510 --> 00:32:50,350
Nevertheless, I cannot grant your request.

288
00:32:51,060 --> 00:32:53,480
But, I'll tell you what I will do.

289
00:32:53,890 --> 00:32:58,440
I'll allow you to keep those fine boots, which normally I would have for myself.

290
00:32:59,360 --> 00:33:02,110
The next town is only five miles away.

291
00:33:02,570 --> 00:33:04,950
And I suggest you now start walking.

292
00:33:05,110 --> 00:33:07,110
Mightn't I keep my horse?

293
00:33:07,610 --> 00:33:09,240
I would like to oblige you...

294
00:33:09,410 --> 00:33:13,790
...but, people like us must be able to travel faster than our clients.

295
00:33:14,330 --> 00:33:15,790
Good day, young sir.

296
00:33:30,140 --> 00:33:32,640
You can put down your hands now, Mr. Barry.

297
00:34:03,300 --> 00:34:07,920
Gale's Regiment of Foot, commanded by Lieutenant General Charles Gale...

298
00:34:08,630 --> 00:34:12,970
...which has distinguished itself during the recent troubles...

299
00:34:13,430 --> 00:34:17,100
...wants several men to supply the places of those veterans...

300
00:34:17,270 --> 00:34:21,900
...who have deserved to be pensioned at one shilling a day.

301
00:34:22,900 --> 00:34:25,980
All clever young fellows who are free and able...

302
00:34:26,280 --> 00:34:30,910
...and are ambitious of becoming gentlemen by bearing arms...

303
00:34:31,200 --> 00:34:34,280
...are hereby invited to meet the Recruiting Officer...

304
00:34:34,830 --> 00:34:38,040
...who promises that they shall meet with every encouragement...

305
00:34:38,210 --> 00:34:41,500
...that merit and good behavior can entitle them to.

306
00:34:42,170 --> 00:34:45,880
Those meeting the qualifications will immediately receive...

307
00:34:46,130 --> 00:34:49,470
...His Majesty's Royal Bounty of one and a half guineas...

308
00:34:49,800 --> 00:34:52,840
...with complete clothing, arms and accoutrements.

309
00:34:54,100 --> 00:34:56,720
King George and Old England forever.

310
00:35:02,190 --> 00:35:05,570
Left, left, left, right, left.

311
00:35:06,150 --> 00:35:09,570
Left, left, left, right, left.

312
00:35:09,820 --> 00:35:11,860
For a young man in difficulty...

313
00:35:12,200 --> 00:35:16,830
...who had killed a man in a duel and was anxious to find refuge from the Law...

314
00:35:17,370 --> 00:35:20,540
...the chance to earn distinction in the European wars...

315
00:35:20,870 --> 00:35:23,130
...seemed a stroke of good fortune.

316
00:35:24,080 --> 00:35:28,050
And King George needed men too much to heed from whence they came.

317
00:35:47,860 --> 00:35:49,650
Hey, lad! Lad!

318
00:35:50,740 --> 00:35:51,860
Lad!

319
00:35:52,280 --> 00:35:55,240
Can I have a new beaker? This one is full of grease.

320
00:36:06,380 --> 00:36:10,710
Covered in grease! Give the gentleman a towel and a basin of turtle soup.

321
00:36:45,620 --> 00:36:48,840
If you want to vex him, ask him about his wife...

322
00:36:49,000 --> 00:36:51,090
...the washerwoman, who beats him.

323
00:36:51,630 --> 00:36:55,550
Mr. Toole, is it a towel of your wife's washing?

324
00:36:56,010 --> 00:36:58,640
They say she wipes your face often with one.

325
00:37:00,260 --> 00:37:03,730
Ask him why he wouldn't see her yesterday when she came here.

326
00:37:04,060 --> 00:37:08,310
Mr. Toole, why did you hide when Mrs. Toole came to visit you?

327
00:37:08,560 --> 00:37:10,650
Afraid of getting your ears boxed?

328
00:37:15,990 --> 00:37:20,450
Gentlemen, you may fight it out with fists if you choose.

329
00:37:21,280 --> 00:37:23,910
We'll form a square for that purpose.

330
00:37:35,260 --> 00:37:37,760
Gentlemen, step this way, please.

331
00:37:44,720 --> 00:37:45,890
Shake hands.

332
00:37:47,270 --> 00:37:48,400
Shake hands.

333
00:37:49,400 --> 00:37:51,020
Take your stance.

334
00:37:53,650 --> 00:37:55,780
No biting, kicking or scratching.

335
00:37:55,940 --> 00:37:58,530
The last man to remain standing is the winner.

336
00:37:59,070 --> 00:38:02,330
Gentlemen, commence fighting, now!

337
00:40:21,710 --> 00:40:25,010
Barry's training continued at the camp.

338
00:40:25,260 --> 00:40:28,560
Within a month he was transformed into a proper young soldier.

339
00:40:31,270 --> 00:40:34,810
The Regiment's strength was steadily increased...

340
00:40:35,150 --> 00:40:37,360
...by the arrival of other troops...

341
00:40:37,650 --> 00:40:41,150
...in preparation for joining their armies fighting in Germany.

342
00:40:43,860 --> 00:40:45,110
One occasion...

343
00:40:45,530 --> 00:40:49,740
...brought the welcome appearance of his second in the fatal duel...

344
00:40:50,660 --> 00:40:52,040
...Captain Grogan.

345
00:41:10,430 --> 00:41:14,020
It would have helped if we'd known what had become of you.

346
00:41:15,600 --> 00:41:18,230
Didn't you think of writing to your mother?

347
00:41:21,940 --> 00:41:23,070
Of course.

348
00:41:25,110 --> 00:41:27,740
But the shame I felt of losing her money...

349
00:41:28,950 --> 00:41:31,120
...my father's sword and pistols....

350
00:41:32,910 --> 00:41:34,700
I couldn't tell her.

351
00:41:36,370 --> 00:41:39,000
She wouldn't care about those things.

352
00:41:39,380 --> 00:41:41,090
You are her only concern.

353
00:41:41,590 --> 00:41:44,840
Tonight you must write her a proper letter.

354
00:41:45,090 --> 00:41:48,380
Tell her that you're safe and well.

355
00:41:51,140 --> 00:41:52,180
I will.

356
00:42:00,650 --> 00:42:02,110
Is Miss Brady well?

357
00:42:07,740 --> 00:42:10,200
There are only six Miss Bradys now.

358
00:42:13,660 --> 00:42:15,790
Has something happened to Nora?

359
00:42:18,160 --> 00:42:20,960
She took on so about your going away...

360
00:42:22,250 --> 00:42:25,170
...that she had to console herself with a husband.

361
00:42:26,010 --> 00:42:29,130
She is now Mrs. John Quin.

362
00:42:33,180 --> 00:42:34,470
Mrs. John Quin?

363
00:42:36,180 --> 00:42:37,980
Was there another John Quin?

364
00:42:38,770 --> 00:42:39,770
No.

365
00:42:39,940 --> 00:42:43,060
The same, me boy. He recovered from his wound.

366
00:42:45,570 --> 00:42:50,200
The shot you hit him with could not hurt him. It was made of tow.

367
00:42:52,200 --> 00:42:53,200
Tow?

368
00:42:55,660 --> 00:42:59,540
The Bradys wouldn't let you kill 1, 500 a year out of the family.

369
00:43:01,620 --> 00:43:06,050
The plan of the duel was arranged in order to get you out of the way.

370
00:43:06,880 --> 00:43:09,510
The cowardly Quin couldn't be brought to marry...

371
00:43:10,010 --> 00:43:11,720
...from fear of you.

372
00:43:11,880 --> 00:43:14,600
But hit him you certainly did, me lad.

373
00:43:15,430 --> 00:43:17,850
With a fine thick plugget of tow.

374
00:43:19,560 --> 00:43:22,600
He was so frightened, it took him an hour to come to.

375
00:43:26,650 --> 00:43:28,360
Are you in want of cash?

376
00:43:30,610 --> 00:43:34,950
You may draw on me. For I got a couple of hundred from your uncle.

377
00:43:35,990 --> 00:43:38,540
While that lasts, you shall never want.

378
00:43:43,080 --> 00:43:47,750
It would require a great historian to explain the causes...

379
00:43:47,920 --> 00:43:51,090
...of the Seven Years' War in Europe...

380
00:43:52,010 --> 00:43:55,090
...to which Barry's regiment was now on its way.

381
00:43:56,390 --> 00:44:00,140
Let it suffice to say, that England and Prussia were allies...

382
00:44:00,730 --> 00:44:02,390
...and at war against...

383
00:44:02,600 --> 00:44:05,900
...France, Sweden, Russia and Austria.

384
00:44:20,450 --> 00:44:22,210
Barry's first taste of battle...

385
00:44:22,370 --> 00:44:25,580
...was only a skirmish against a rearguard of Frenchmen...

386
00:44:26,170 --> 00:44:28,750
...who occupied an orchard beside a road...

387
00:44:28,920 --> 00:44:32,590
...down which the English main force wished to pass.

388
00:44:34,220 --> 00:44:37,140
Though this encounter is not recorded in any history book...

389
00:44:38,010 --> 00:44:40,890
...it was memorable enough for those who took part.

390
00:47:02,660 --> 00:47:05,450
I've only a hundred guineas left to give you...

391
00:47:06,160 --> 00:47:08,500
...for I lost the rest at cards.

392
00:47:12,670 --> 00:47:15,840
Kiss me, me boy, for we'll never meet again.

393
00:47:48,950 --> 00:47:53,250
It is well to dream of glorious war in a snug armchair.

394
00:47:54,540 --> 00:47:57,550
It is a different thing to see it firsthand.

395
00:47:59,050 --> 00:48:03,510
After his friend's death, Barry's thoughts turned from military glory...

396
00:48:04,760 --> 00:48:07,350
...to those of escaping the Service...

397
00:48:07,510 --> 00:48:10,180
...to which he was tied for six years.

398
00:48:24,410 --> 00:48:27,200
Gentlemen talk of the Age of Chivalry...

399
00:48:28,080 --> 00:48:32,210
...but remember the ploughmen, poachers and pickpockets they lead.

400
00:48:33,290 --> 00:48:37,630
It is with these sad instruments great warriors and kings...

401
00:48:38,250 --> 00:48:41,210
...have been doing their murderous work in the world.

402
00:48:51,100 --> 00:48:54,560
Barry could not have fallen into worse circumstances...

403
00:48:54,770 --> 00:48:57,190
...than those in which he found himself.

404
00:48:58,020 --> 00:49:01,820
But fate did not intend he should remain long an English soldier.

405
00:49:02,650 --> 00:49:04,240
An accident occurred...

406
00:49:04,530 --> 00:49:08,030
...which took him out of the Service in a rather singular manner.

407
00:49:36,730 --> 00:49:40,690
Freddie, I hope you won't be angry with me, but...

408
00:49:41,480 --> 00:49:45,110
...I've got something to tell you which you won't be happy about.

409
00:49:45,320 --> 00:49:47,030
Oh? What is it?

410
00:49:47,660 --> 00:49:50,950
First, promise me you'll keep your temper.

411
00:49:52,120 --> 00:49:56,160
Jonathan, don't be an ass! You're making a big mystery of it.

412
00:49:56,910 --> 00:49:58,420
What is going on?

413
00:49:59,710 --> 00:50:01,670
I have to go away again.

414
00:50:02,460 --> 00:50:04,090
For about two weeks.

415
00:50:04,760 --> 00:50:07,550
Oh, God, you're not serious?

416
00:50:08,470 --> 00:50:11,930
I am. There's nothing I can do about it.

417
00:50:13,140 --> 00:50:14,600
Where to this time?

418
00:50:15,100 --> 00:50:19,440
To Bremen, carrying important dispatches to Prince Henry.

419
00:50:20,520 --> 00:50:24,730
But you promised me last time it would be never again.

420
00:50:25,280 --> 00:50:28,860
I know. And I've kept my part of the bargain, but...

421
00:50:29,610 --> 00:50:32,990
...Pontersby insists I'm the only one who can be entrusted with the trip.

422
00:50:33,160 --> 00:50:37,460
Here was the opportunity to escape from the Army... ...for which he had been searching.

423
00:50:39,040 --> 00:50:43,840
It was only a few miles to the area occupied by their Prussian allies...

424
00:50:45,090 --> 00:50:47,590
...where this Officer's uniform and papers...

425
00:50:47,760 --> 00:50:50,260
...should allow him to travel without suspicion...

426
00:50:50,760 --> 00:50:54,760
...and stay ahead of the news of his desertion.

427
00:50:55,810 --> 00:50:58,100
Then, we'll have little time together.

428
00:50:59,230 --> 00:51:00,690
Are you cross with me?

429
00:51:02,310 --> 00:51:06,110
Damn you, I can't stay cross with you for long.

430
00:51:08,240 --> 00:51:09,700
Oh, Jonathan...

431
00:51:10,570 --> 00:51:14,030
...at times like this I realize how much I care for you...

432
00:51:14,410 --> 00:51:17,620
...and how empty life would be without you.

433
00:51:18,120 --> 00:51:19,290
Frederick!

434
00:51:43,480 --> 00:51:48,730
Barry was glad to see the uniforms of the Prussian Infantry.

435
00:51:49,860 --> 00:51:53,990
They showed him that he was out of English-occupied territory.

436
00:52:00,620 --> 00:52:03,080
His intention was to make for Holland...

437
00:52:03,250 --> 00:52:06,670
...almost the only neutral country of Europe, in those times...

438
00:52:07,290 --> 00:52:09,800
...and thence to get a passage home, somehow.

439
00:52:10,970 --> 00:52:12,300
As he rode away...

440
00:52:12,800 --> 00:52:15,760
...Barry felt he was now in his proper sphere.

441
00:52:16,470 --> 00:52:20,470
And determined never again to fall from the rank of a gentleman.

442
00:52:39,700 --> 00:52:41,080
Excuse me, miss.

443
00:52:42,160 --> 00:52:43,210
Yes.

444
00:52:43,790 --> 00:52:44,830
Good day.

445
00:52:45,670 --> 00:52:46,710
Good day.

446
00:52:48,710 --> 00:52:50,300
Do you speak English?

447
00:52:52,970 --> 00:52:54,970
I'm speaking little.

448
00:52:58,470 --> 00:53:02,220
I have not eaten all day, no food.

449
00:53:03,520 --> 00:53:06,810
Is there an inn nearby where I might have a meal?

450
00:53:08,940 --> 00:53:10,980
No...I don't think so.

451
00:53:12,990 --> 00:53:17,410
There is nothing to eat before Grunberg.

452
00:53:20,990 --> 00:53:22,540
Do you live near here?

453
00:53:25,960 --> 00:53:27,000
Yes.

454
00:53:30,500 --> 00:53:35,090
Would you...feed me? I'd be happy to pay you.

455
00:53:39,350 --> 00:53:40,550
I think so.

456
00:54:05,910 --> 00:54:07,540
Is it a boy or girl?

457
00:54:08,870 --> 00:54:09,880
A boy.

458
00:54:13,050 --> 00:54:14,380
What's his name?

459
00:54:14,590 --> 00:54:15,630
Peter.

460
00:54:17,010 --> 00:54:18,300
How old is he?

461
00:54:19,550 --> 00:54:21,050
He's one year old.

462
00:54:24,850 --> 00:54:26,520
Where is Peter's father?

463
00:54:29,020 --> 00:54:30,810
Where he is?

464
00:54:32,900 --> 00:54:33,940
Yes.

465
00:54:35,360 --> 00:54:36,650
In the war.

466
00:54:41,740 --> 00:54:43,450
How long has he been gone?

467
00:54:46,450 --> 00:54:49,040
Sorry...I didn't understand.

468
00:54:50,120 --> 00:54:51,170
What?

469
00:54:54,880 --> 00:54:56,550
Oh! A long time.

470
00:54:58,260 --> 00:54:59,880
Since springtime.

471
00:55:06,020 --> 00:55:08,310
It must be hard for you to be alone.

472
00:55:12,400 --> 00:55:13,480
It is.

473
00:55:19,280 --> 00:55:22,570
It must be dangerous for you in the war.

474
00:55:27,870 --> 00:55:30,000
I'm an Officer and must do my duty.

475
00:55:40,470 --> 00:55:42,010
You are sometimes...

476
00:55:42,340 --> 00:55:43,640
...lonely?

477
00:55:46,560 --> 00:55:47,680
Sometimes.

478
00:56:05,240 --> 00:56:07,830
What did you say your name is?

479
00:56:10,080 --> 00:56:11,580
Lieutenant Fakenham.

480
00:56:12,710 --> 00:56:14,170
No, I mean...

481
00:56:15,250 --> 00:56:17,880
...what is the name before Fakenham?

482
00:56:19,760 --> 00:56:21,050
My first name?

483
00:56:22,510 --> 00:56:23,550
Jonathan.

484
00:56:25,760 --> 00:56:26,800
Jonathan?

485
00:56:28,930 --> 00:56:30,310
Would you like...

486
00:56:30,470 --> 00:56:31,890
...to stay with me?

487
00:56:32,600 --> 00:56:35,600
For a few days, or sometimes?

488
00:56:39,690 --> 00:56:41,400
That would be very nice.

489
00:57:12,390 --> 00:57:14,520
Goodbye, my beautiful Lischen.

490
00:57:21,440 --> 00:57:24,030
Goodbye, Redmond.

491
00:57:27,110 --> 00:57:28,240
I love you.

492
00:57:28,450 --> 00:57:29,620
I love you.

493
00:57:31,580 --> 00:57:33,040
Look after yourself.

494
00:57:37,670 --> 00:57:39,000
God be with you.

495
00:57:44,210 --> 00:57:47,890
A lady who sets her heart on a lad in uniform...

496
00:57:48,800 --> 00:57:51,810
...must prepare to change lovers pretty quickly...

497
00:57:51,970 --> 00:57:54,140
...or her life will be a sad one.

498
00:57:56,140 --> 00:57:59,980
This heart of Lischen's was like many a neighboring town...

499
00:58:01,190 --> 00:58:05,690
...and had been stormed and occupied many times before Barry came to invest it.

500
00:58:31,140 --> 00:58:33,680
During five years of war...

501
00:58:34,720 --> 00:58:39,190
...the illustrious Frederick had so exhausted the males of his kingdom...

502
00:58:39,730 --> 00:58:42,020
...that he had to employ recruiters...

503
00:58:42,440 --> 00:58:45,730
...who would commit any crime, including kidnapping...

504
00:58:46,070 --> 00:58:50,530
...to keep supplied those brilliant regiments of his with cannon fodder.

505
00:59:23,110 --> 00:59:24,480
Good evening, sir.

506
00:59:26,190 --> 00:59:29,990
I'm Captain Potzdorf. To whom have I the honor of speaking?

507
00:59:31,660 --> 00:59:35,450
Good evening. I'm Lieutenant Fakenham, Gale's Regiment of Foot.

508
00:59:35,870 --> 00:59:37,330
Pleased to meet you.

509
00:59:38,830 --> 00:59:40,960
Can we be of assistance to you?

510
00:59:42,170 --> 00:59:46,210
Thank you, but I am carrying urgent dispatches and must continue on my way.

511
00:59:47,300 --> 00:59:48,800
And your destination?

512
00:59:50,800 --> 00:59:51,800
Bremen.

513
00:59:54,140 --> 00:59:57,260
Then you're obviously lost, Lieutenant.

514
00:59:57,560 --> 00:59:59,640
Bremen is in the opposite direction.

515
00:59:59,810 --> 01:00:00,810
Are you sure?

516
01:00:00,980 --> 01:00:02,020
Yes.

517
01:00:03,900 --> 01:00:05,400
Wouldn't you know it!

518
01:00:06,150 --> 01:00:10,940
My departure was so hasty that my orderly forgot to prepare proper maps.

519
01:00:12,450 --> 01:00:13,740
I understand.

520
01:00:16,370 --> 01:00:18,910
Please, do not be offended, Lieutenant...

521
01:00:20,000 --> 01:00:22,580
...but are you carrying your identity papers?

522
01:00:24,830 --> 01:00:26,130
Yes, of course.

523
01:00:27,840 --> 01:00:29,130
May I see them?

524
01:00:32,420 --> 01:00:33,510
Of course.

525
01:00:38,220 --> 01:00:39,260
Here you are.

526
01:00:39,430 --> 01:00:40,470
Thank you.

527
01:00:54,490 --> 01:00:58,200
Thank you, Lieutenant, I hope I haven't inconvenienced you.

528
01:00:59,160 --> 01:01:00,330
Not at all.

529
01:01:02,700 --> 01:01:05,500
Now that we are riding in the same direction...

530
01:01:06,420 --> 01:01:10,590
...may I offer you a meal and a bed for the night...

531
01:01:11,340 --> 01:01:13,510
...and a proper map for the journey?

532
01:01:16,050 --> 01:01:20,560
That's kind of you, Captain. I'd be honored to accept.

533
01:01:43,580 --> 01:01:46,210
Barry was treated with great civility...

534
01:01:47,040 --> 01:01:49,710
...and was asked questions about England.

535
01:01:50,210 --> 01:01:54,720
He answered as best he could, inventing many stories.

536
01:01:56,260 --> 01:01:58,260
He described the King and Ministers...

537
01:01:59,180 --> 01:02:02,600
...boasted that the British Ambassador in Berlin was his uncle...

538
01:02:03,020 --> 01:02:05,980
...and even offered Potzdorf a letter of introduction.

539
01:02:07,690 --> 01:02:10,560
His host seemed satisfied with these stories.

540
01:02:11,400 --> 01:02:16,950
But, he led Barry on with a skillful combination of questions...

541
01:02:17,110 --> 01:02:18,530
...and flattery.

542
01:02:18,740 --> 01:02:22,030
I know so little of your country of England...

543
01:02:22,950 --> 01:02:25,750
...except that you are the bravest nation in the world...

544
01:02:25,910 --> 01:02:28,960
...and that we are fortunate to have such allies.

545
01:02:30,920 --> 01:02:32,500
Lieutenant Fakenham...

546
01:02:33,420 --> 01:02:37,130
...let us drink to the friendship of our two great nations.

547
01:02:38,760 --> 01:02:40,720
To our two great nations.

548
01:03:00,820 --> 01:03:04,790
Aren't you lucky, going to Bremen tomorrow?

549
01:03:06,240 --> 01:03:09,040
I know one of the loveliest women in Europe there.

550
01:03:11,290 --> 01:03:13,250
Would you take a letter to her?

551
01:03:15,050 --> 01:03:16,170
Certainly.

552
01:03:16,550 --> 01:03:19,930
By the way, to whom are you carrying your dispatches?

553
01:03:23,220 --> 01:03:24,640
General Williamson.

554
01:03:27,270 --> 01:03:28,480
General Williamson?

555
01:03:28,640 --> 01:03:29,680
Yes.

556
01:03:31,140 --> 01:03:33,980
General Percival Williamson?

557
01:03:36,150 --> 01:03:37,400
Yes, the same.

558
01:03:44,240 --> 01:03:45,780
Sergeant.

559
01:03:50,370 --> 01:03:52,170
This man is under arrest.

560
01:03:56,590 --> 01:03:59,710
Under arrest? Captain Potzdorf, sir...

561
01:04:00,670 --> 01:04:02,470
...I'm a British Officer.

562
01:04:03,090 --> 01:04:05,680
You're a liar and an imposter.

563
01:04:06,970 --> 01:04:08,430
You're a deserter.

564
01:04:09,770 --> 01:04:13,770
I suspected you this morning. Your lies and folly have confirmed this.

565
01:04:14,980 --> 01:04:19,110
You pretend to carry dispatches to a General, dead these ten months.

566
01:04:19,860 --> 01:04:21,900
You say the British Ambassador in Berlin...

567
01:04:22,110 --> 01:04:25,410
...is your uncle, with the ridiculous name of O'Grady.

568
01:04:27,450 --> 01:04:30,910
Now, will you join and take the bounty, or be given up?

569
01:04:35,670 --> 01:04:36,960
I volunteer.

570
01:04:40,800 --> 01:04:44,590
The Prussian Service was worse than the English.

571
01:04:45,800 --> 01:04:49,260
The life of a Private Soldier was frightful.

572
01:04:51,640 --> 01:04:55,770
Punishment was incessant. Every Officer had the right to inflict it.

573
01:04:58,400 --> 01:05:02,190
The gauntlet was a common penalty for minor offenses.

574
01:05:03,360 --> 01:05:07,740
More serious ones were punishable by mutilation or death.

575
01:05:16,830 --> 01:05:19,960
At the close of the Seven Years' War the army...

576
01:05:20,630 --> 01:05:25,930
...renowned for its disciplined valor, was officered by native Prussians.

577
01:05:26,720 --> 01:05:28,850
But it was mostly composed...

578
01:05:29,550 --> 01:05:32,180
...of men from the lowest levels of humanity...

579
01:05:32,890 --> 01:05:36,310
...hired or stolen from every nation in Europe.

580
01:05:38,190 --> 01:05:42,650
Thus, Barry fell into the worst company...

581
01:05:44,280 --> 01:05:48,410
...and was soon far advanced in the science of every kind of misconduct.

582
01:07:47,030 --> 01:07:48,110
Help.

583
01:07:48,860 --> 01:07:50,570
Get me out of here.

584
01:09:07,270 --> 01:09:11,730
The Colonel's speech declared the King's satisfaction...

585
01:09:12,490 --> 01:09:15,610
...with the conduct of the Regiment at the Battle of Audorf...

586
01:09:15,990 --> 01:09:20,620
...and with Corporal Barry's bravery in rescuing Captain Potzdorf...

587
01:09:21,700 --> 01:09:25,710
...which was to be rewarded with the sum of two frederick d'or.

588
01:09:31,210 --> 01:09:34,340
Corporal Barry, eight paces forward...

589
01:09:35,510 --> 01:09:36,550
...march!

590
01:09:49,060 --> 01:09:50,360
Corporal Barry.

591
01:09:55,110 --> 01:09:58,240
You're a gallant soldier, and evidently of good stock...

592
01:09:58,780 --> 01:10:01,240
...but you're idle and unprincipled.

593
01:10:01,910 --> 01:10:03,950
You're a bad influence on the men.

594
01:10:04,750 --> 01:10:08,460
And for all your bravery, I'm sure you'll come to no good.

595
01:10:11,790 --> 01:10:14,460
I hope the Colonel is mistaken.

596
01:10:15,590 --> 01:10:20,050
I have fallen into bad company, but I've only done as other soldiers do.

597
01:10:20,470 --> 01:10:23,180
I've never had a friend or protector before...

598
01:10:23,350 --> 01:10:26,140
...to show that I was worthy of better things.

599
01:10:27,310 --> 01:10:30,650
The Colonel may say I'm ruined, and send me to the Devil.

600
01:10:30,900 --> 01:10:34,150
But, I would go to the Devil to serve the Regiment.

601
01:10:38,450 --> 01:10:41,030
Corporal Barry, fall in.

602
01:10:51,330 --> 01:10:56,210
The war ended and Barry's regiment was garrisoned in the Capital.

603
01:10:58,300 --> 01:11:03,640
He had, for some time, ingratiated himself with Captain Potzdorf...

604
01:11:04,470 --> 01:11:07,560
...whose confidence in him was about to bring its reward.

605
01:11:17,360 --> 01:11:18,650
Good morning, Redmond.

606
01:11:18,820 --> 01:11:20,410
Good morning, Captain.

607
01:11:21,570 --> 01:11:25,580
I should like you to meet my uncle, the Minister of Police.

608
01:11:26,830 --> 01:11:28,790
Good morning, Herr Minister.

609
01:11:31,000 --> 01:11:32,170
Redmond...

610
01:11:32,330 --> 01:11:36,800
...I've spoken to the Minister regarding your services and your fortune is made.

611
01:11:37,340 --> 01:11:39,340
We shall get you out of the Army...

612
01:11:39,510 --> 01:11:43,140
...appoint you to the Police Bureau, and, in time...

613
01:11:44,140 --> 01:11:47,930
...we'll allow you to move in a better sphere.

614
01:11:49,680 --> 01:11:51,140
Thank you, Captain.

615
01:11:54,310 --> 01:11:59,570
Your loyalty to me and service to the Regiment has pleased me.

616
01:12:01,320 --> 01:12:04,910
Now there is another occasion on which you can assist us.

617
01:12:06,370 --> 01:12:07,830
If you succeed...

618
01:12:08,580 --> 01:12:09,870
...depend on it...

619
01:12:10,410 --> 01:12:12,170
...your reward will be secure.

620
01:12:14,000 --> 01:12:15,630
I'll do my best, sir.

621
01:12:24,640 --> 01:12:29,680
There is a gentleman in Berlin in the service of the Empress of Austria.

622
01:12:30,310 --> 01:12:33,650
He calls himself the Chevalier de Balibari.

623
01:12:34,730 --> 01:12:37,400
He appears to be a professional gambler.

624
01:12:38,690 --> 01:12:40,070
He's a libertine:

625
01:12:40,860 --> 01:12:43,110
Fond of women, of good food...

626
01:12:44,030 --> 01:12:45,990
...polished, obliging.

627
01:12:48,200 --> 01:12:50,660
He speaks French and German indifferently.

628
01:12:51,830 --> 01:12:55,460
But we have reason to fancy that Monsieur de Balibari...

629
01:12:56,540 --> 01:12:59,130
...is a native of your country of Ireland.

630
01:13:01,210 --> 01:13:04,050
And that he has come here as a spy.

631
01:13:15,400 --> 01:13:17,400
Your knowledge of English...

632
01:13:17,730 --> 01:13:21,190
...makes you an ideal choice to go into his service...

633
01:13:22,190 --> 01:13:24,820
...and find out whether or not he is a spy.

634
01:13:28,030 --> 01:13:30,330
Does this assignment interest you?

635
01:13:32,290 --> 01:13:36,580
Minister, I'm interested in anything that can be of service to you...

636
01:13:36,750 --> 01:13:38,420
...and Captain Potzdorf.

637
01:13:50,100 --> 01:13:52,560
You will not know a word of English.

638
01:13:53,850 --> 01:13:58,810
If the Chevalier remarks on your accent, say you are Hungarian.

639
01:13:59,940 --> 01:14:01,570
You served in the war.

640
01:14:02,070 --> 01:14:05,070
You left the Army for medical reasons...

641
01:14:06,740 --> 01:14:10,030
...then served Monsieur de Quellenberg for two years.

642
01:14:10,740 --> 01:14:14,540
He's now with the Army in Silesia, but you'll have a certificate from him.

643
01:14:39,730 --> 01:14:41,440
Good morning, Your Honor.

644
01:15:00,250 --> 01:15:03,500
So you are the young man recommended by Seebach.

645
01:15:05,460 --> 01:15:08,930
Yes, Your Honor, here are my credentials.

646
01:15:19,600 --> 01:15:21,900
Your name is Lazlo Zilagy?

647
01:15:24,770 --> 01:15:26,230
Yes, Your Honor.

648
01:15:29,240 --> 01:15:32,320
Monsieur de Quellenberg recommends you highly.

649
01:15:34,660 --> 01:15:36,910
Monsieur is a very good man.

650
01:15:45,090 --> 01:15:46,880
It was imprudent of him...

651
01:15:47,630 --> 01:15:50,630
...but when Barry saw the Chevalier's splendor...

652
01:15:51,590 --> 01:15:53,260
...and noble manner...

653
01:15:53,800 --> 01:15:56,600
...he felt unable to keep disguise with him.

654
01:15:58,430 --> 01:16:00,600
Those who have never been exiled...

655
01:16:01,350 --> 01:16:04,810
...know not what it is to hear a friendly voice in captivity...

656
01:16:05,940 --> 01:16:09,610
...and would not understand the cause...

657
01:16:09,780 --> 01:16:12,910
...of the burst of feeling now about to take place.

658
01:16:16,450 --> 01:16:18,620
You seem the right one to me.

659
01:16:22,250 --> 01:16:23,880
Thank you, Your Honor.

660
01:16:30,840 --> 01:16:32,260
Are you ill?

661
01:16:38,140 --> 01:16:39,140
Sir...

662
01:16:39,890 --> 01:16:42,600
...I have a confession to make to you.

663
01:16:44,190 --> 01:16:45,520
I'm an Irishman.

664
01:16:46,940 --> 01:16:48,650
My name is Redmond Barry.

665
01:16:52,150 --> 01:16:54,820
I was abducted into the Prussian Army.

666
01:16:56,320 --> 01:16:58,660
Now I've been put into your service...

667
01:16:59,490 --> 01:17:04,120
...by my Captain Potzdorf and his uncle, the Minister of Police...

668
01:17:07,840 --> 01:17:11,340
...to serve as a watch upon your...actions...

669
01:17:13,420 --> 01:17:17,760
...and to give information to them.

670
01:17:38,700 --> 01:17:44,210
The Chevalier was much affected... ...at thus finding one of his countrymen.

671
01:17:45,160 --> 01:17:47,500
For he too was an exile.

672
01:17:48,290 --> 01:17:50,590
And a friendly voice, a look...

673
01:17:51,500 --> 01:17:54,470
...brought the old country back to his memory.

674
01:17:58,470 --> 01:18:01,600
He is very religious and attends church regularly.

675
01:18:02,010 --> 01:18:04,850
After Mass he comes home for breakfast.

676
01:18:09,190 --> 01:18:11,570
He then takes an airing in his carriage.

677
01:18:11,730 --> 01:18:15,110
Barry presented his reports regularly to the Minister.

678
01:18:16,200 --> 01:18:19,700
The details were arranged beforehand with the Chevalier.

679
01:18:21,200 --> 01:18:24,080
He was instructed to tell the truth...

680
01:18:24,870 --> 01:18:27,830
...as much as his story would possibly bear.

681
01:18:29,500 --> 01:18:33,800
The information he gave was very minute and accurate...

682
01:18:34,340 --> 01:18:36,170
...though not very important.

683
01:18:37,380 --> 01:18:39,470
--But who does not acknowledge him.

684
01:18:57,610 --> 01:18:59,410
Wine or punch, Your Honor?

685
01:19:00,160 --> 01:19:01,200
Wine.

686
01:19:02,280 --> 01:19:06,040
It was agreed that Barry should keep his character of valet.

687
01:19:07,250 --> 01:19:11,040
That, before strangers, he should not know a word of English.

688
01:19:12,080 --> 01:19:16,760
And that he should keep a lookout on the trumps when serving the wine.

689
01:19:18,880 --> 01:19:20,680
Having excellent eyesight...

690
01:19:21,430 --> 01:19:23,220
...and a natural aptitude...

691
01:19:24,220 --> 01:19:27,810
...he was able to give his dear patron much assistance...

692
01:19:28,100 --> 01:19:30,900
...against his opponents at the green table.

693
01:19:32,520 --> 01:19:35,650
If, for instance, he wiped the table with a napkin...

694
01:19:36,150 --> 01:19:38,740
...the enemy was strong in Diamonds.

695
01:19:39,740 --> 01:19:42,990
If he adjusted a chair it meant Ace King.

696
01:19:43,160 --> 01:19:46,580
If he said, "Punch or wine, My Lord?"...

697
01:19:46,740 --> 01:19:48,950
...Hearts were meant, and so forth.

698
01:20:00,970 --> 01:20:02,720
The Prince of Tubingen...

699
01:20:02,890 --> 01:20:06,560
...who had intimate connections with the Great Frederick...

700
01:20:08,220 --> 01:20:12,190
...was passionately fond of play as were the gentlemen...

701
01:20:12,350 --> 01:20:14,360
...of almost all the Courts of Europe.

702
01:20:14,560 --> 01:20:17,780
You owe 15,500 frederick d'or.

703
01:20:52,350 --> 01:20:53,480
Chevalier...

704
01:20:57,480 --> 01:20:59,400
...though I cannot say how...

705
01:21:01,070 --> 01:21:03,240
...I believe you have cheated me.

706
01:21:11,370 --> 01:21:13,460
I deny Your Grace's accusation...

707
01:21:14,250 --> 01:21:17,040
...and beg you to say how you have been cheated.

708
01:21:19,590 --> 01:21:20,750
I don't know.

709
01:21:21,760 --> 01:21:23,510
But I believe I have been.

710
01:21:25,680 --> 01:21:29,890
Your Grace owes me 15,500 frederick d'or...

711
01:21:30,890 --> 01:21:32,890
...which I have honorably won.

712
01:21:35,140 --> 01:21:36,270
Chevalier...

713
01:21:38,900 --> 01:21:42,440
...if you will have your money now you must fight for it.

714
01:21:44,610 --> 01:21:46,320
If you will be patient...

715
01:21:47,620 --> 01:21:50,330
...maybe I will pay you something another time.

716
01:21:54,250 --> 01:21:58,880
Your Grace, if I am to be so tame as to take this...

717
01:21:59,630 --> 01:22:03,010
...then I must give up an honorable and lucrative occupation.

718
01:22:05,050 --> 01:22:07,220
I have said all there is to be said.

719
01:22:08,640 --> 01:22:11,930
I am at your disposal for whatever purposes you wish.

720
01:22:14,270 --> 01:22:15,430
Good night.

721
01:22:37,710 --> 01:22:39,460
Was the Prince cheated?

722
01:22:41,960 --> 01:22:45,760
In as far as I am able to tell, Herr Minister, no.

723
01:22:46,130 --> 01:22:48,300
I believe he won the money fairly.

724
01:22:52,810 --> 01:22:55,020
What are the Chevalier's intentions?

725
01:22:56,180 --> 01:22:57,390
I'm not sure.

726
01:22:57,940 --> 01:23:02,400
The Prince told him that if he wanted his money he'd have to fight for it.

727
01:23:05,940 --> 01:23:08,650
A meeting with the Prince is impossible.

728
01:23:12,780 --> 01:23:15,200
The Prince has left him no other choice.

729
01:23:40,600 --> 01:23:42,310
Now.

730
01:23:42,480 --> 01:23:46,570
Will you be able to return here tomorrow without arousing suspicion?

731
01:23:48,400 --> 01:23:51,200
I know they won't allow a meeting with the Prince.

732
01:23:51,490 --> 01:23:55,910
But if I say that, do you know any reason why he'll pay me what he owes?

733
01:23:57,540 --> 01:24:00,660
You must tell them I intend to demand satisfaction.

734
01:24:01,790 --> 01:24:03,920
Don't look so downcast, my boy.

735
01:24:04,500 --> 01:24:08,460
They cannot harm me, the Austrian Embassy will see to that.

736
01:24:09,130 --> 01:24:13,430
The worst they can do is send me out of this dreary country of theirs.

737
01:24:14,760 --> 01:24:17,720
If they should, don't worry...

738
01:24:18,640 --> 01:24:20,640
...you shall not be left behind.

739
01:24:21,310 --> 01:24:22,850
Have no fear of that.

740
01:24:25,020 --> 01:24:28,820
The King has determined to send the Chevalier out of the country.

741
01:24:29,940 --> 01:24:32,400
Has he already demanded satisfaction?

742
01:24:34,200 --> 01:24:37,330
Not yet, but I believe he intends to...

743
01:24:38,490 --> 01:24:39,870
...possibly today.

744
01:24:40,870 --> 01:24:42,920
Then this must be done tomorrow.

745
01:24:43,370 --> 01:24:45,330
All the arrangements are made.

746
01:24:50,420 --> 01:24:54,380
You said he takes a drive after breakfast every day.

747
01:24:55,720 --> 01:24:56,760
Yes, sir.

748
01:24:57,970 --> 01:25:01,140
Is there any reason he should do any different tomorrow?

749
01:25:02,520 --> 01:25:03,560
No, sir.

750
01:25:04,230 --> 01:25:05,270
Good.

751
01:25:06,940 --> 01:25:10,070
When the Chevalier comes out to his carriage in the morning...

752
01:25:10,320 --> 01:25:13,650
...two Officers will meet him and escort him to the frontier.

753
01:25:14,570 --> 01:25:16,740
His baggage will be sent after him.

754
01:25:17,070 --> 01:25:18,200
Excellent.

755
01:25:30,460 --> 01:25:32,760
At ten o'clock the next morning...

756
01:25:33,420 --> 01:25:35,550
...the Chevalier de Balibari...

757
01:25:36,140 --> 01:25:38,930
...went out for his regular morning drive.

758
01:25:48,440 --> 01:25:50,230
Where's my servant, Lazlo?

759
01:25:50,690 --> 01:25:53,150
I will let down the steps, Your Honor.

760
01:26:04,080 --> 01:26:05,210
What is this about?

761
01:26:05,370 --> 01:26:06,790
Please get inside.

762
01:26:09,460 --> 01:26:10,960
Am I under arrest?

763
01:26:11,500 --> 01:26:13,460
We're driving to the frontier.

764
01:26:14,550 --> 01:26:18,220
Frontier? But I'm on my way to the Austrian Ambassador's house.

765
01:26:19,140 --> 01:26:21,680
My orders are to escort you to the frontier...

766
01:26:21,850 --> 01:26:24,140
...and see you safely across the border.

767
01:26:24,600 --> 01:26:26,390
But, I'm not going to the frontier.

768
01:26:26,560 --> 01:26:30,610
I have very important business at the Austrian Ambassador's house.

769
01:26:31,150 --> 01:26:35,440
My orders are to take Your Honor to the frontier by any means necessary.

770
01:26:36,070 --> 01:26:37,360
If you come willingly...

771
01:26:37,530 --> 01:26:40,660
...I'm to give you this purse on behalf of the Prince of Tubingen...

772
01:26:40,820 --> 01:26:42,740
...containing 2,000 frederick d'or.

773
01:26:52,840 --> 01:26:54,800
All Europe shall hear of this.

774
01:27:01,140 --> 01:27:04,770
And so, without papers or passport...

775
01:27:05,890 --> 01:27:08,690
...and under the eyes of two Prussian Officers...

776
01:27:09,020 --> 01:27:13,650
...Barry was escorted across the frontier into Saxony and freedom.

777
01:27:15,360 --> 01:27:19,950
The Chevalier himself had uneventfully crossed the frontier the night before.

778
01:27:32,460 --> 01:27:36,920
By these wonderful circumstances, Barry was once more free...

779
01:27:38,090 --> 01:27:40,720
...and began his professional work as a gamester...

780
01:27:41,590 --> 01:27:46,770
...resolving, thenceforward and forever, to live the life of a gentleman.

781
01:27:48,890 --> 01:27:50,190
The four wins.

782
01:27:51,480 --> 01:27:55,610
Soon he and the Chevalier were received in all the Courts of Europe...

783
01:27:56,780 --> 01:27:58,110
...and were speedily...

784
01:27:58,280 --> 01:28:01,740
...in the very best society where play was patronized...

785
01:28:02,200 --> 01:28:05,450
...and professors of that science always welcome.

786
01:28:08,830 --> 01:28:09,960
The seven.

787
01:28:10,710 --> 01:28:12,120
Why not the seven?

788
01:28:13,960 --> 01:28:15,290
All...all, yes.

789
01:28:20,340 --> 01:28:21,800
No more bets.

790
01:28:27,890 --> 01:28:28,930
Number seven...

791
01:28:29,310 --> 01:28:30,350
...loses.

792
01:28:38,940 --> 01:28:40,400
Place your bets.

793
01:28:43,490 --> 01:28:47,370
Chevalier, will you give me credit for 5,000 louis d'or, please?

794
01:28:48,450 --> 01:28:50,000
Of course, Lord Ludd.

795
01:29:02,050 --> 01:29:03,300
Five thousand.

796
01:29:05,550 --> 01:29:07,510
Now, everything on the four.

797
01:29:11,180 --> 01:29:13,940
Yes, I know, everything on the four.

798
01:29:25,860 --> 01:29:27,160
No more bets.

799
01:29:33,580 --> 01:29:35,040
The four loses.

800
01:29:49,300 --> 01:29:50,890
It is not important.

801
01:29:52,560 --> 01:29:55,020
Now, I'm weary.

802
01:29:55,940 --> 01:29:58,860
I would like dinner. Shall we?

803
01:30:01,530 --> 01:30:03,360
Excuse me, Lord Ludd.

804
01:30:04,820 --> 01:30:06,200
If you don't mind.

805
01:30:07,450 --> 01:30:08,530
Not at all.

806
01:30:11,080 --> 01:30:16,000
They always played on credit with any person of honor or noble lineage.

807
01:30:17,120 --> 01:30:19,210
They never pressed for their winnings...

808
01:30:19,460 --> 01:30:22,380
...or declined to receive promissory notes.

809
01:30:23,960 --> 01:30:27,260
But woe to the man who did not pay when the note became due.

810
01:30:28,050 --> 01:30:31,140
Barry was sure to wait upon him with his bill.

811
01:30:31,850 --> 01:30:33,520
There were few bad debts.

812
01:30:33,680 --> 01:30:34,730
Saluez.

813
01:30:35,390 --> 01:30:39,060
It was Barry's skill with the sword, and readiness to use it...

814
01:30:39,520 --> 01:30:42,650
...that maintained the reputation of the firm, so to speak.

815
01:30:42,820 --> 01:30:43,820
En garde!

816
01:31:34,330 --> 01:31:36,240
I will pay you today, sir.

817
01:31:37,910 --> 01:31:40,040
Thus, it will be seen, their life...

818
01:31:40,710 --> 01:31:44,290
...for all its splendor, was not without danger and difficulty...

819
01:31:45,550 --> 01:31:48,300
...requiring talent and determination for success.

820
01:31:49,470 --> 01:31:52,930
And required them to live a wandering and disconnected life.

821
01:31:54,760 --> 01:31:58,980
And, though they were swimming upon the high tide of fortune...

822
01:31:59,480 --> 01:32:03,690
...and prospering with the cards, they had little to show for their labor...

823
01:32:03,940 --> 01:32:06,570
...but some fine clothes and a few trinkets.

824
01:32:19,450 --> 01:32:24,500
Five years in the Army, and considerable experience of the world...

825
01:32:25,130 --> 01:32:29,510
...had dispelled any romantic notions regarding love...

826
01:32:30,090 --> 01:32:32,260
...with which Barry commenced life.

827
01:32:32,930 --> 01:32:37,060
And he had it in mind, as many gentlemen had done before him...

828
01:32:37,680 --> 01:32:40,850
...to marry a woman of fortune and condition.

829
01:32:42,020 --> 01:32:44,440
And, as such things so often happen...

830
01:32:44,810 --> 01:32:49,110
...these thoughts coincided with his setting sight upon a lady...

831
01:32:49,440 --> 01:32:53,490
...who will play a considerable part in the drama of his life.

832
01:32:54,950 --> 01:32:56,910
The Countess of Lyndon...

833
01:32:57,780 --> 01:33:00,080
...Viscountess Bullingdon of England...

834
01:33:00,250 --> 01:33:02,660
...Baroness Castle Lyndon of Ireland.

835
01:33:03,920 --> 01:33:06,960
A woman of vast wealth and great beauty.

836
01:33:09,550 --> 01:33:13,130
She was the wife of Sir Charles Lyndon...

837
01:33:13,470 --> 01:33:15,010
...Knight of the Bath...

838
01:33:15,180 --> 01:33:18,970
...Minister to George III at several of the Courts of Europe.

839
01:33:19,970 --> 01:33:22,430
A cripple, wheeled about in a chair...

840
01:33:23,180 --> 01:33:26,440
...worn out by gout and a myriad of diseases.

841
01:33:28,230 --> 01:33:30,690
Her Ladyship's Chaplain, Mr. Runt...

842
01:33:31,150 --> 01:33:35,240
...acted as tutor to her son, the little Viscount Bullingdon...

843
01:33:36,110 --> 01:33:39,660
...a melancholy little boy, much attached to his mother.

844
01:35:58,800 --> 01:36:01,130
I'm going outside for a breath of air.

845
01:36:01,760 --> 01:36:03,050
Yes, My Lady.

846
01:37:59,210 --> 01:38:01,500
To make a long story short...

847
01:38:02,260 --> 01:38:06,380
...six hours after they met... Her Ladyship was in love.

848
01:38:07,430 --> 01:38:09,760
And once Barry got into her company...

849
01:38:10,260 --> 01:38:13,850
...he found innumerable occasions to improve his intimacy...

850
01:38:15,100 --> 01:38:18,100
...and was scarcely out of Her Ladyship's sight.

851
01:39:11,620 --> 01:39:13,160
Good evening, gentlemen.

852
01:39:13,330 --> 01:39:14,790
Good evening.

853
01:39:16,120 --> 01:39:17,290
Sir Charles.

854
01:39:20,000 --> 01:39:21,790
Good evening, Mr. Barry.

855
01:39:22,750 --> 01:39:24,710
Have you done with my Lady?

856
01:39:27,260 --> 01:39:28,300
Pardon?

857
01:39:29,260 --> 01:39:34,140
Come, sir. I'm a man who would rather be known as a cuckold than a fool.

858
01:39:36,980 --> 01:39:40,140
I think, Sir Charles, that you've had too much to drink.

859
01:39:41,350 --> 01:39:42,400
Ha, ha, what?

860
01:39:42,690 --> 01:39:48,030
As it happens, your Chaplain, Mr. Runt, introduced me to your Lady...

861
01:39:48,360 --> 01:39:52,200
...to advise me on a religious matter, of which she is an expert.

862
01:39:58,960 --> 01:40:02,920
He wants...to step into my shoes.

863
01:40:04,290 --> 01:40:07,090
He wants to step into my shoes.

864
01:40:09,420 --> 01:40:13,050
Is it not a pleasure for me, as I am drawing near the goal...

865
01:40:13,300 --> 01:40:15,890
...to find my home such a happy one...

866
01:40:16,390 --> 01:40:20,560
...my wife so fond of me, that she is even now thinking of appointing a successor?

867
01:40:20,730 --> 01:40:23,980
Isn’t it a comfort to see her like a prudent housewife...

868
01:40:24,150 --> 01:40:26,730
...getting everything ready for my departure?

869
01:40:28,690 --> 01:40:31,820
I hope you're not thinking of leaving us, Sir Charles?

870
01:40:34,120 --> 01:40:37,490
Not so soon as you may fancy, perhaps.

871
01:40:37,660 --> 01:40:41,370
I've been given over many times these four years.

872
01:40:41,750 --> 01:40:44,500
And there was always a candidate or two...

873
01:40:44,670 --> 01:40:47,500
...waiting to apply for the situation.

874
01:40:47,960 --> 01:40:49,460
I'm sorry for you, Mr. Barry.

875
01:40:49,630 --> 01:40:52,380
It grieves me to keep you or any gentleman waiting.

876
01:40:52,550 --> 01:40:54,720
Had you not better arrange with my doctor...

877
01:40:54,930 --> 01:40:58,470
...or have the cook flavor my omelet with arsenic, eh?

878
01:40:59,140 --> 01:41:04,600
What are the odds, gentlemen, that I live to see Mr. Barry hang yet?

879
01:41:08,270 --> 01:41:11,780
Sir, let those laugh that win.

880
01:41:14,610 --> 01:41:15,740
Gentlemen.

881
01:41:37,510 --> 01:41:38,970
I'll get a surgeon.

882
01:41:43,890 --> 01:41:45,850
Have some brandy, Sir Charles.

883
01:41:46,190 --> 01:41:48,860
From a report

884
01:41:50,530 --> 01:41:53,530
"Died at Spa in Belgium...

885
01:41:54,200 --> 01:41:57,490
"...Sir Charles Reginald Lyndon...

886
01:41:58,200 --> 01:42:01,120
"...Knight of the Bath, Member of Parliament...

887
01:42:01,370 --> 01:42:02,790
"...and for many years...

888
01:42:02,950 --> 01:42:06,830
"...His Majesty's Representative at various European Courts.

889
01:42:08,380 --> 01:42:12,670
"He has left behind him a name which is endeared to all his friends."

890
01:43:03,890 --> 01:43:05,270
"Dearly beloved...

891
01:43:06,600 --> 01:43:09,900
"...we are gathered together here in the sight of God...

892
01:43:10,770 --> 01:43:13,400
"...and in the face of this congregation...

893
01:43:14,480 --> 01:43:18,240
"...to join together this man and this woman in holy matrimony."

894
01:43:18,400 --> 01:43:21,620
A year later, on the fifteenth of June...

895
01:43:22,160 --> 01:43:24,120
...in the year 1773...

896
01:43:25,330 --> 01:43:30,250
...Redmond Barry had the honor to lead to the altar the Countess of Lyndon.

897
01:43:32,040 --> 01:43:36,880
The ceremony was performed by the Reverend Runt, Her Ladyship's Chaplain.

898
01:43:37,760 --> 01:43:40,840
"And therefore is not in any way to be enterprised...

899
01:43:41,640 --> 01:43:44,810
"...nor taken in hand unadvisedly...

900
01:43:45,890 --> 01:43:48,180
"...lightly or wantonly...

901
01:43:49,890 --> 01:43:53,610
"...to satisfy men's carnal lusts and appetites...

902
01:43:53,900 --> 01:43:56,980
"...like brute beasts that have no understanding.

903
01:44:00,490 --> 01:44:01,820
"But reverently...

904
01:44:02,990 --> 01:44:04,330
"...discreetly...

905
01:44:05,280 --> 01:44:06,660
"...advisedly...

906
01:44:07,830 --> 01:44:09,040
"...soberly...

907
01:44:10,330 --> 01:44:12,290
"...and in the fear of God.

908
01:44:14,460 --> 01:44:18,510
"Duly considering the causes for which matrimony was ordained.

909
01:44:20,420 --> 01:44:21,550
"First...

910
01:44:22,180 --> 01:44:26,310
"...it was ordained for the procreation of children to be brought up...

911
01:44:26,470 --> 01:44:30,640
"...in the fear and nurture of the Lord, and to the praise of His holy name.

912
01:44:31,980 --> 01:44:33,190
"Secondly...

913
01:44:33,810 --> 01:44:39,030
"...it was ordained for a remedy against sin, and to avoid fornication...

914
01:44:40,280 --> 01:44:41,860
"...that such persons...."

915
01:44:42,030 --> 01:44:45,410
Barry had now arrived at the pitch of prosperity...

916
01:44:46,450 --> 01:44:51,620
...and by his own energy had raised himself to a higher sphere of society...

917
01:44:52,870 --> 01:44:57,840
...having procured His Majesty's gracious permission to add the name...

918
01:44:58,170 --> 01:45:00,380
...of his lovely Lady to his own.

919
01:45:01,470 --> 01:45:05,640
Thenceforth, Redmond Barry assumed the style...

920
01:45:05,800 --> 01:45:08,100
...and title of Barry Lyndon.

921
01:45:40,210 --> 01:45:43,720
Redmond, would you mind not smoking for a while?

922
01:45:49,810 --> 01:45:51,060
Redmond?

923
01:45:59,690 --> 01:46:04,780
Lady Lyndon was soon destined to occupy a place in Barry's life...

924
01:46:05,570 --> 01:46:09,910
...not very much more important than the elegant carpets and pictures...

925
01:46:10,410 --> 01:46:14,000
...which would form the pleasant background of his existence.

926
01:46:23,920 --> 01:46:27,430
My Lord Bullingdon, you seem particularly glum today.

927
01:46:32,060 --> 01:46:35,310
You should be happy that your mother has remarried.

928
01:46:39,610 --> 01:46:40,940
Not in this way.

929
01:46:43,820 --> 01:46:45,440
And not in such haste.

930
01:46:47,610 --> 01:46:49,740
And certainly not to this man.

931
01:46:53,160 --> 01:46:55,660
I think you judge your mother too harshly.

932
01:46:57,460 --> 01:47:00,290
Do you not like your new father?

933
01:47:02,250 --> 01:47:03,550
Not very much.

934
01:47:05,010 --> 01:47:08,090
He seems to me little more than a common opportunist.

935
01:47:09,260 --> 01:47:11,720
I don't think he loves my mother at all.

936
01:47:12,810 --> 01:47:16,640
And it hurts me to see her make such a fool of herself.

937
01:47:37,000 --> 01:47:41,460
At the end of a year Her Ladyship presented Barry with a son.

938
01:47:42,590 --> 01:47:45,670
Bryan Patrick Lyndon, they called him.

939
01:47:49,550 --> 01:47:54,640
None of those ancient heroes E'er saw a cannon ball

940
01:47:54,810 --> 01:47:59,350
Or knew the force of powder To slay their foes withal

941
01:47:59,520 --> 01:48:04,440
But our brave boys do know it And banish all their fears

942
01:48:04,610 --> 01:48:09,360
With a tow, row, row, row, row To the British Grenadiers

943
01:48:09,530 --> 01:48:14,030
Her Ladyship and Barry lived, after a while, pretty separate.

944
01:48:15,780 --> 01:48:20,710
She preferred quiet, or to say the truth, he preferred it for her...

945
01:48:21,330 --> 01:48:25,540
...being a great friend to a modest and tranquil behavior in woman.

946
01:48:27,380 --> 01:48:31,220
Besides, she was a mother, and would have great comfort...

947
01:48:31,380 --> 01:48:35,300
...in the dressing, educating and dandling of their little Bryan.

948
01:48:36,260 --> 01:48:39,230
For whose sake it was fit, Barry believed...

949
01:48:39,560 --> 01:48:43,440
...that she should give up the pleasures and frivolities of the world...

950
01:48:43,600 --> 01:48:47,270
...leaving that part of the duty of every family of distinction...

951
01:48:47,440 --> 01:48:49,740
...to be performed by him.

952
01:50:07,350 --> 01:50:11,610
Lady Lyndon tended to a melancholy and maudlin temper...

953
01:50:12,650 --> 01:50:16,820
...and, left alone by her husband, was rarely happy or in good humor.

954
01:50:17,990 --> 01:50:21,580
Now she must add jealousy to her other complaints...

955
01:50:22,240 --> 01:50:25,330
...and find rivals even among her maids.

956
01:50:55,280 --> 01:50:57,570
Samuel, what would the time be?

957
01:51:00,870 --> 01:51:03,370
Twenty-five minutes past eleven, My Lady.

958
01:51:17,380 --> 01:51:19,680
Shall we make this the last game, ladies?

959
01:51:19,840 --> 01:51:20,890
Yes.

960
01:52:08,810 --> 01:52:10,390
Good morning, ladies.

961
01:52:10,940 --> 01:52:12,270
Good morning, sir.

962
01:52:14,110 --> 01:52:17,780
Would you mind excusing us? I'd like a word alone with Lady Lyndon.

963
01:52:43,720 --> 01:52:44,800
I'm sorry.

964
01:53:28,560 --> 01:53:31,350
This coat is made of the finest velvet...

965
01:53:31,930 --> 01:53:34,850
...all cunningly worked with silver thread.

966
01:53:35,190 --> 01:53:39,690
No finer velvet has ever been woven, and you will see none better anywhere.

967
01:53:40,360 --> 01:53:41,900
Pardon me, gentlemen.

968
01:53:45,410 --> 01:53:47,200
Good morning, dearest.

969
01:53:48,530 --> 01:53:52,410
We're taking the children for a ride to the village. We'll be back for tea.

970
01:53:52,580 --> 01:53:54,870
Have a nice time. I'll see you then.

971
01:53:55,080 --> 01:53:56,880
Goodbye, little Bryan.

972
01:54:01,380 --> 01:54:02,800
Lord Bullingdon.

973
01:54:05,180 --> 01:54:07,090
Take good care of your mother.

974
01:54:12,810 --> 01:54:15,440
Come now, give your father a proper kiss.

975
01:54:26,200 --> 01:54:27,570
Lord Bullingdon...

976
01:54:28,410 --> 01:54:31,200
...is that the way to behave to your father?

977
01:54:35,120 --> 01:54:37,620
Lord Bullingdon, have you lost your tongue?

978
01:54:40,210 --> 01:54:45,720
My father was Sir Charles Lyndon. I have not forgotten him, if others have.

979
01:54:46,930 --> 01:54:49,680
Lord Bullingdon, you have insulted your father!

980
01:54:51,350 --> 01:54:53,810
Madam, you have insulted my father.

981
01:54:58,690 --> 01:55:02,980
Dearest, would you excuse us? We have something to discuss in private.

982
01:55:03,320 --> 01:55:04,610
Gentlemen.

983
01:55:17,120 --> 01:55:18,170
One.

984
01:55:21,500 --> 01:55:22,540
Two.

985
01:55:26,220 --> 01:55:27,300
Three.

986
01:55:30,680 --> 01:55:31,930
Four.

987
01:55:34,970 --> 01:55:36,100
Five.

988
01:55:38,980 --> 01:55:40,060
Six.

989
01:55:49,240 --> 01:55:50,700
Lord Bullingdon...

990
01:55:51,990 --> 01:55:55,490
...I have always been willing to live with you on friendly terms.

991
01:55:55,910 --> 01:55:57,830
But be clear about one thing:

992
01:55:59,160 --> 01:56:01,710
As men serve me, I serve them.

993
01:56:03,000 --> 01:56:05,550
I never laid a cane on the back of a Lord before...

994
01:56:05,710 --> 01:56:09,680
...but, if you force me to, I shall speedily become used to the practice.

995
01:56:12,260 --> 01:56:14,680
Do you have anything to say for yourself?

996
01:56:17,020 --> 01:56:18,020
No.

997
01:56:21,650 --> 01:56:22,810
You may go.

998
01:56:23,770 --> 01:56:28,150
Barry believed, and not without some reason, that it had been...

999
01:56:28,400 --> 01:56:32,530
...a declaration of war against him by Bullingdon from the start...

1000
01:56:33,160 --> 01:56:35,780
...and that the evil consequences that ensued...

1001
01:56:35,950 --> 01:56:38,410
...were entirely of Bullingdon's creating.

1002
01:56:41,540 --> 01:56:44,210
I shall make you into a real magician now, Bryan.

1003
01:56:44,380 --> 01:56:47,590
I shall show you the knot that never was.

1004
01:56:48,340 --> 01:56:51,090
As Bullingdon grew up to be a man...

1005
01:56:52,050 --> 01:56:55,100
...his hatred for Barry assumed an intensity...

1006
01:56:55,800 --> 01:56:59,100
...equaled only by his increased devotion to his mother.

1007
01:56:59,350 --> 01:57:01,440
Very good, Bryan. A little bow.

1008
01:57:04,650 --> 01:57:06,900
Put it on the table for me. Thank you.

1009
01:57:07,190 --> 01:57:13,530
For Bryan's eighth birthday the local nobility, gentry and their children...

1010
01:57:13,860 --> 01:57:15,570
...came to pay their respects.

1011
01:57:15,740 --> 01:57:18,870
The inside and outside are quite empty.

1012
01:57:19,290 --> 01:57:22,120
Wave your hand over the top. Is there anything there?

1013
01:57:22,290 --> 01:57:23,370
Yes? Oh!

1014
01:57:24,420 --> 01:57:27,590
Wonderful! Wonderful, colorful silk handkerchiefs!

1015
01:57:27,750 --> 01:57:30,420
Take a bow, Bryan, you did that beautifully.

1016
01:57:30,670 --> 01:57:31,760
Very good.

1017
01:57:32,260 --> 01:57:34,760
Let's see if you have something behind your ear.

1018
01:57:34,930 --> 01:57:36,010
Yes, you have. Ha, ha.

1019
01:57:36,600 --> 01:57:38,350
A little ball. Let's make it vanish.

1020
01:57:38,510 --> 01:57:39,560
It's gone.

1021
01:57:39,720 --> 01:57:42,180
Here it is, behind my elbow.

1022
01:57:44,520 --> 01:57:47,270
Wave your hand over my green silk handkerchief...

1023
01:57:47,440 --> 01:57:51,320
...and see if we can produce a magic flower. I wonder if we can?

1024
01:57:52,190 --> 01:57:53,490
Here it comes.

1025
01:57:54,990 --> 01:57:56,740
The colors of the rainbow.

1026
01:57:57,120 --> 01:58:01,450
You know all the colors of the rainbow produce but one color, Bryan.

1027
01:58:01,950 --> 01:58:03,710
Nothing in my magic cabinet.

1028
01:58:03,870 --> 01:58:05,870
They produce the color...white.

1029
01:58:06,040 --> 01:58:07,960
And there is my own...

1030
01:58:08,500 --> 01:58:11,130
...beautiful white rabbit.

1031
01:58:13,170 --> 01:58:16,130
Bryan, you did that very well. A little bow.

1032
01:58:40,330 --> 01:58:43,950
We crept up on their fort, and I jumped over the wall first.

1033
01:58:44,160 --> 01:58:45,960
My fellows jumped after me.

1034
01:58:46,500 --> 01:58:50,960
You should have seen the Frenchmen's faces when 23 rampaging he-devils...

1035
01:58:51,670 --> 01:58:56,300
...sword and pistol, cut and thrust, came tumbling into their fort.

1036
01:58:57,760 --> 01:59:00,890
In three minutes we left as many Artillerymen's heads...

1037
01:59:01,050 --> 01:59:02,850
...as there were cannonballs.

1038
01:59:04,470 --> 01:59:07,560
Later we were visited by our noble Prince Henry.

1039
01:59:08,520 --> 01:59:11,980
"Who is the man who has done this?" I stepped forward.

1040
01:59:13,190 --> 01:59:15,690
"How many heads was it that you cut off?" He says.

1041
01:59:15,860 --> 01:59:18,610
"Nineteen," says I, "besides wounding several."

1042
01:59:19,820 --> 01:59:23,790
Well, I'll be blessed, if he didn't burst into tears.

1043
01:59:24,540 --> 01:59:26,500
"Noble, noble fellow," he said.

1044
01:59:26,710 --> 01:59:31,000
"Here is nineteen golden guineas, one for each head that you cut off."

1045
01:59:31,790 --> 01:59:33,380
What do you think of that?

1046
01:59:33,550 --> 01:59:35,800
Were you allowed to keep the heads?

1047
01:59:36,720 --> 01:59:39,470
No, they always become the property of the King.

1048
01:59:40,090 --> 01:59:42,050
Will you tell me another story?

1049
01:59:42,550 --> 01:59:44,350
I'll tell you one tomorrow.

1050
01:59:44,510 --> 01:59:46,810
Will you play cards with me tomorrow?

1051
01:59:47,430 --> 01:59:49,560
Of course I will. Now go to sleep.

1052
01:59:53,940 --> 01:59:55,900
Will you keep the candles lit?

1053
01:59:56,230 --> 01:59:59,450
Bryan, big boys don't sleep with the candles lit.

1054
02:00:00,280 --> 02:00:02,070
I'm afraid of the dark.

1055
02:00:02,320 --> 02:00:04,990
My darling, there's nothing to be afraid of.

1056
02:00:06,160 --> 02:00:08,580
But, I like it with the candles lit.

1057
02:00:08,750 --> 02:00:11,670
All right, you can sleep with the candles lit.

1058
02:00:12,830 --> 02:00:14,170
Thank you, Papa.

1059
02:00:14,630 --> 02:00:15,750
Good night.

1060
02:00:38,110 --> 02:00:39,280
Ah, Redmond!

1061
02:00:40,360 --> 02:00:45,780
It's a blessing to see my darling boy has attained a position I knew was his due.

1062
02:00:47,870 --> 02:00:50,870
And for which I pinched myself to educate him.

1063
02:00:52,920 --> 02:00:54,880
Little Bryan is a darling boy...

1064
02:00:55,960 --> 02:00:57,960
...and you live in great splendor.

1065
02:00:58,800 --> 02:01:00,920
Your lady wife knows she has a treasure...

1066
02:01:01,090 --> 02:01:03,840
...she couldn't have had, had she married a Duke.

1067
02:01:06,510 --> 02:01:09,810
But, if she should tire of my wild Redmond...

1068
02:01:10,560 --> 02:01:12,890
...and his old-fashioned Irish ways...

1069
02:01:14,650 --> 02:01:16,360
...or if she should die...

1070
02:01:17,320 --> 02:01:20,690
...what future would there be for my son, and my grandson?

1071
02:01:22,610 --> 02:01:24,700
You have not a penny of your own...

1072
02:01:26,320 --> 02:01:29,580
...and cannot transact any business without her signature.

1073
02:01:31,330 --> 02:01:35,920
Upon her death the entire estate would go to young Bullingdon...

1074
02:01:37,710 --> 02:01:40,050
...who bears you little affection.

1075
02:01:41,630 --> 02:01:43,720
You could be penniless tomorrow...

1076
02:01:44,010 --> 02:01:47,970
...and darling Bryan at the mercy of his stepbrother.

1077
02:01:52,390 --> 02:01:54,230
Shall I tell you something?

1078
02:01:55,600 --> 02:02:00,070
There is only one way for you and your son to have real security.

1079
02:02:01,150 --> 02:02:03,700
You must obtain a title.

1080
02:02:05,490 --> 02:02:09,240
I shall not rest until I see you Lord Lyndon.

1081
02:02:12,250 --> 02:02:14,410
You have important friends.

1082
02:02:14,580 --> 02:02:16,920
They can tell you how these things are done.

1083
02:02:18,040 --> 02:02:21,630
For money, well-timed and properly applied...

1084
02:02:22,630 --> 02:02:24,590
...can accomplish anything.

1085
02:02:27,510 --> 02:02:32,560
And Barry was acquainted with someone... ...who knew how these things were done.

1086
02:02:33,600 --> 02:02:37,900
This was the distinguished Barrister and former Government Minister...

1087
02:02:38,560 --> 02:02:39,900
...Lord Hallam...

1088
02:02:40,440 --> 02:02:44,900
...whose acquaintance he had made, like so many others, at the gaming table.

1089
02:02:46,240 --> 02:02:50,030
Do you happen to know the Thirteenth Earl of Wendover?

1090
02:02:51,580 --> 02:02:53,080
I don't believe I do.

1091
02:02:53,240 --> 02:02:57,620
Well, this nobleman is one of the Gentlemen of His Majesty's Closet...

1092
02:02:58,460 --> 02:03:03,090
...with whom our revered Monarch is on terms of considerable intimacy.

1093
02:03:04,760 --> 02:03:08,470
In my opinion, you would be wise to fix upon him...

1094
02:03:09,090 --> 02:03:14,060
...your chief reliance for the advancement of your claim to the peerage.

1095
02:03:16,430 --> 02:03:20,560
When I take up a person, Mr. Lyndon, he, or she, is safe.

1096
02:03:21,440 --> 02:03:23,820
There is no question about them anymore.

1097
02:03:24,230 --> 02:03:28,820
My friends are the best people. I don't mean they're the most virtuous...

1098
02:03:29,780 --> 02:03:33,080
...or, indeed, the least virtuous, or the cleverest...

1099
02:03:33,280 --> 02:03:36,120
...or the stupidest, richest or best born.

1100
02:03:36,790 --> 02:03:37,960
But, the best.

1101
02:03:38,120 --> 02:03:41,750
In a word, people about whom there is no question.

1102
02:03:43,710 --> 02:03:46,300
I cannot promise how long it will take.

1103
02:03:46,840 --> 02:03:49,800
You can appreciate it is not an easy matter.

1104
02:03:50,590 --> 02:03:54,600
But, any gentleman with an estate, and 30,000 a year...

1105
02:03:55,390 --> 02:03:57,100
...should have a peerage.

1106
02:04:01,600 --> 02:04:04,820
And there standing behind me was a total stranger.

1107
02:04:05,280 --> 02:04:07,610
I looked at him, and he said to me:

1108
02:04:08,030 --> 02:04:13,200
"Excuse me, sir, could you tell me, is Lord Wendover alive or dead?"

1109
02:04:15,330 --> 02:04:17,750
I was so astonished, I couldn't think of what to say.

1110
02:04:17,910 --> 02:04:21,210
Then I became angry, and said to him, "He's dead."

1111
02:04:21,580 --> 02:04:26,630
The striving after this peerage was one of Barry's most unlucky dealings.

1112
02:04:28,130 --> 02:04:30,800
He made great sacrifices to bring it about.

1113
02:04:32,050 --> 02:04:34,890
He lavished money here, and diamonds there.

1114
02:04:36,510 --> 02:04:39,060
He bought lands at ten times their value...

1115
02:04:39,350 --> 02:04:43,560
...purchased pictures and articles of virtue at ruinous prices.

1116
02:04:45,020 --> 02:04:48,610
He gave entertainments to those friends to his claim...

1117
02:04:49,320 --> 02:04:53,070
...who, being about the Royal person, were likely to advance it.

1118
02:04:54,530 --> 02:04:59,540
And, I can tell you, bribes were administered. And in high places, too.

1119
02:05:01,040 --> 02:05:05,590
So near the person of His Majesty that you would be astonished to know...

1120
02:05:06,130 --> 02:05:09,960
...what great noblemen condescended to receive his loans.

1121
02:05:13,640 --> 02:05:15,760
This is by Ludovico Cordi...

1122
02:05:16,600 --> 02:05:18,720
...a disciple of Alessandro Allori.

1123
02:05:20,060 --> 02:05:22,310
It's dated 1605...

1124
02:05:23,390 --> 02:05:25,730
...and shows, "The Adoration of The Magi."

1125
02:05:28,480 --> 02:05:29,690
It's beautiful.

1126
02:05:29,860 --> 02:05:30,900
Yes.

1127
02:05:32,320 --> 02:05:35,320
I love the use of the color blue by the artist.

1128
02:05:35,820 --> 02:05:37,740
Yes, that is very beautiful.

1129
02:05:40,370 --> 02:05:42,330
What is the price of this one?

1130
02:05:44,210 --> 02:05:46,460
Well, this is one of my best pictures.

1131
02:05:47,250 --> 02:05:52,050
But, if you really like it, I'm sure we can come to some arrangement.

1132
02:05:56,760 --> 02:05:58,010
Count Andrachier.

1133
02:05:58,180 --> 02:05:59,350
Count Andrachier.

1134
02:05:59,510 --> 02:06:00,970
Mr. Henry Drummond.

1135
02:06:01,140 --> 02:06:02,230
Mr. Drummond.

1136
02:06:02,390 --> 02:06:03,980
Sir Gilbert Elliot, Your Majesty.

1137
02:06:04,140 --> 02:06:05,400
Sir Gilbert.

1138
02:06:05,560 --> 02:06:07,060
Lord Wendover, Your Majesty.

1139
02:06:07,230 --> 02:06:09,570
I'm glad to see you here today, Lord Wendover.

1140
02:06:09,730 --> 02:06:11,610
What news of Lady Wendover?

1141
02:06:11,780 --> 02:06:14,780
Thank you, Your Majesty. Lady Wendover is much better.

1142
02:06:14,950 --> 02:06:19,450
Good! Present my compliments to her. Say we miss her company here.

1143
02:06:19,620 --> 02:06:20,870
Thank you, Your Majesty.

1144
02:06:21,040 --> 02:06:23,000
And what of those excellent boys of yours?

1145
02:06:23,160 --> 02:06:27,830
They're well. Charles has gone to sea under Captain Geary on the Ramillies.

1146
02:06:28,130 --> 02:06:31,210
John has gone to Oxford to learn how to preach and pray.

1147
02:06:31,380 --> 02:06:32,460
Good, good!

1148
02:06:33,510 --> 02:06:36,590
Your Majesty, may I present Mr. Barry Lyndon.

1149
02:06:36,760 --> 02:06:40,050
Mr. Lyndon. We were very fond of Sir Charles Lyndon.

1150
02:06:40,310 --> 02:06:41,850
And how is Lady Lyndon?

1151
02:06:42,020 --> 02:06:43,930
She's very well, Your Majesty.

1152
02:06:44,270 --> 02:06:49,150
Mr. Lyndon raised a company of troops to fight in America against the rebels.

1153
02:06:49,770 --> 02:06:53,570
Good, Mr. Lyndon. Raise another company and go with them, too.

1154
02:06:54,440 --> 02:06:55,990
Sir Christopher Neville.

1155
02:06:56,150 --> 02:06:57,660
Sir Christopher.

1156
02:06:57,820 --> 02:06:58,910
Peregrine Cavendish.

1157
02:06:59,070 --> 02:07:00,120
Mr. Peregrine.

1158
02:07:06,460 --> 02:07:10,380
Barry was born clever enough at gaining a fortune...

1159
02:07:10,960 --> 02:07:13,000
...but incapable of keeping one.

1160
02:07:14,590 --> 02:07:18,720
For the qualities and energies which lead a man to achieve the first...

1161
02:07:19,090 --> 02:07:22,390
...are often the very cause of his ruin in the latter case.

1162
02:07:24,180 --> 02:07:28,100
Now he was burdened with the harassing cares and responsibilities...

1163
02:07:28,850 --> 02:07:32,110
...which are the dismal adjuncts of great rank and property.

1164
02:07:33,190 --> 02:07:34,780
And his life now...

1165
02:07:35,490 --> 02:07:39,990
...seemed to consist mostly of drafts of letters to lawyers and money-brokers...

1166
02:07:40,160 --> 02:07:41,870
...and endless correspondence...

1167
02:07:42,030 --> 02:07:43,870
...with decorators and cooks.

1168
02:08:08,940 --> 02:08:13,230
Gentlemen, I'm going to leave you for a few minutes. Carry on with your work.

1169
02:08:13,520 --> 02:08:14,570
Yes, sir.

1170
02:08:32,460 --> 02:08:33,500
Bully.

1171
02:08:37,170 --> 02:08:38,340
What does...

1172
02:08:40,090 --> 02:08:41,840
..."strenuous" mean?

1173
02:08:45,810 --> 02:08:47,600
Bryan, I'm trying to work.

1174
02:08:50,480 --> 02:08:52,060
But what does it mean?

1175
02:08:56,070 --> 02:08:58,690
It means "an effort requiring strength."

1176
02:09:05,410 --> 02:09:07,370
What does "quadrangle" mean?

1177
02:09:13,790 --> 02:09:17,590
A quadrangle is a four-sided figure like a square or a rectangle.

1178
02:09:18,590 --> 02:09:21,550
Now, please be quiet, and let me get on with my work.

1179
02:09:36,730 --> 02:09:38,610
Bryan, please be quiet!

1180
02:09:42,820 --> 02:09:44,450
Have you seen my pencil?

1181
02:09:47,830 --> 02:09:49,120
No, I haven't.

1182
02:10:03,970 --> 02:10:06,590
Bryan, please stop making so much noise.

1183
02:10:11,180 --> 02:10:12,390
That's my pencil.

1184
02:10:12,560 --> 02:10:13,600
No, it isn't.

1185
02:10:13,770 --> 02:10:15,310
It is. It's my pencil!

1186
02:10:15,480 --> 02:10:16,810
I've had this all morning.

1187
02:10:16,980 --> 02:10:18,190
It's my pencil!

1188
02:10:18,480 --> 02:10:20,190
Listen, will you be quiet!

1189
02:10:20,360 --> 02:10:21,610
It's my pencil!

1190
02:10:23,280 --> 02:10:24,990
I'll teach you a lesson.

1191
02:10:27,620 --> 02:10:29,780
What the devil's going on in here?

1192
02:10:30,990 --> 02:10:34,080
I told you never to lay a hand on this child.

1193
02:10:36,670 --> 02:10:37,710
One.

1194
02:10:41,130 --> 02:10:42,170
Two.

1195
02:10:45,260 --> 02:10:46,550
Three.

1196
02:10:48,890 --> 02:10:50,010
Four.

1197
02:10:52,560 --> 02:10:53,850
Five.

1198
02:10:56,770 --> 02:10:57,810
-Aah! -Six.

1199
02:11:04,740 --> 02:11:07,030
Will that be all, Mr. Redmond Barry?

1200
02:11:08,660 --> 02:11:10,320
Yes, that will be all.

1201
02:11:14,160 --> 02:11:15,830
Well then, look you now.

1202
02:11:17,620 --> 02:11:22,300
From this moment, I will submit to no further chastisement from you.

1203
02:11:24,460 --> 02:11:27,760
I will kill you if you lay hands on me ever again.

1204
02:11:30,430 --> 02:11:32,350
Is that clear to you, sir?

1205
02:11:36,180 --> 02:11:37,520
Get out of here!

1206
02:13:38,390 --> 02:13:41,680
Don't you think he fits my shoes very well, Your Ladyship?

1207
02:13:47,730 --> 02:13:49,070
Dear child...

1208
02:13:50,320 --> 02:13:53,320
...what a pity it is I'm not dead, for your sake.

1209
02:13:55,870 --> 02:13:58,580
The Lyndons would then have a worthy representative...

1210
02:13:59,370 --> 02:14:01,080
...and enjoy all the benefits...

1211
02:14:01,250 --> 02:14:04,370
...of the illustrious blood of the Barrys of Barryville.

1212
02:14:09,090 --> 02:14:10,380
Would they not...

1213
02:14:11,590 --> 02:14:13,090
...Mr. Redmond Barry?

1214
02:14:19,390 --> 02:14:21,600
From the way I love this child...

1215
02:14:21,970 --> 02:14:24,850
...you ought to know how I would've loved his elder brother...

1216
02:14:25,020 --> 02:14:27,730
...had he proved worthy of any mother's affection.

1217
02:14:29,400 --> 02:14:30,440
Madam!

1218
02:14:33,610 --> 02:14:36,320
I have borne as long as mortal could endure...

1219
02:14:36,490 --> 02:14:41,120
...the ill treatment of the insolent Irish upstart whom you've taken to your bed.

1220
02:14:42,410 --> 02:14:47,710
It is not only his lowly birth and the brutality of his manners which disgust me.

1221
02:14:48,540 --> 02:14:51,920
But the shameful nature of his conduct toward Your Ladyship...

1222
02:14:52,090 --> 02:14:55,010
...his brutal and ungentlemanlike behavior...

1223
02:14:55,260 --> 02:14:56,760
...his open infidelity...

1224
02:14:57,220 --> 02:15:01,350
...his shameless robberies and swindling of my property, and yours.

1225
02:15:02,430 --> 02:15:07,440
And as I cannot personally chastise this low-bred ruffian, and cannot bear...

1226
02:15:07,600 --> 02:15:09,360
...to witness his treatment of you...

1227
02:15:09,520 --> 02:15:12,400
...and loathe his company as if it were the plague...

1228
02:15:13,730 --> 02:15:16,700
...I have decided to leave my home and never return.

1229
02:15:17,450 --> 02:15:19,780
At least, during his detested life...

1230
02:15:20,700 --> 02:15:22,160
...or during my own.

1231
02:16:53,710 --> 02:16:54,750
Good day, My Lord.

1232
02:16:54,920 --> 02:16:56,340
Good day, Barker.

1233
02:16:58,840 --> 02:17:00,550
Will anyone be joining Your Lordship?

1234
02:17:00,720 --> 02:17:02,180
No, I shall be alone.

1235
02:17:07,970 --> 02:17:09,270
Thank you.

1236
02:17:13,770 --> 02:17:17,230
The roast beef's very good, My Lord. Uh-huh.

1237
02:17:36,290 --> 02:17:38,380
Hello, Neville. How are you?

1238
02:17:38,840 --> 02:17:40,460
Ah, Barry. Hello.

1239
02:17:40,920 --> 02:17:43,510
I see you're alone. Why don't you join me?

1240
02:17:43,680 --> 02:17:46,800
Oh, thank you, Barry, you're very kind, but...

1241
02:17:47,430 --> 02:17:49,680
...I'm expecting someone to join me.

1242
02:17:50,470 --> 02:17:54,400
What a shame! Lady Lyndon and I have missed your company lately.

1243
02:17:54,810 --> 02:17:57,150
Please give my respects to Lady Lyndon...

1244
02:17:57,310 --> 02:18:00,780
...and say I've been very busy of late and not been able to go about much.

1245
02:18:00,940 --> 02:18:01,990
I shall.

1246
02:18:02,360 --> 02:18:05,990
The eighth of next month we're having some guests over for cards...

1247
02:18:06,160 --> 02:18:09,030
...we'd love to have you and Lady Wendover join us.

1248
02:18:09,910 --> 02:18:14,210
I'll check my diary, but I think I'm engaged on that evening.

1249
02:18:14,750 --> 02:18:17,880
I hope you're not engaged. We'd love to see you again.

1250
02:18:18,340 --> 02:18:20,750
I'll write and say if I'm free or not.

1251
02:18:21,590 --> 02:18:25,050
I look forward to hearing from you. It's nice to see you again.

1252
02:18:29,260 --> 02:18:31,560
If he had murdered Lord Bullingdon...

1253
02:18:31,930 --> 02:18:36,400
...Barry could scarcely have been received with more coldness and resentment...

1254
02:18:36,650 --> 02:18:39,270
...that now followed him in town and country.

1255
02:18:40,730 --> 02:18:42,440
His friends fell away from him.

1256
02:18:43,110 --> 02:18:46,070
A legend arose of his cruelty to his stepson.

1257
02:18:56,870 --> 02:18:59,750
Now all the bills came down on him together.

1258
02:19:01,090 --> 02:19:04,720
All the bills he had been contracting for the years of his marriage...

1259
02:19:04,880 --> 02:19:07,970
...and which the creditors sent in with a hasty unanimity.

1260
02:19:08,550 --> 02:19:10,390
Their amount was frightful.

1261
02:19:11,970 --> 02:19:16,100
Barry was now bound up in an inextricable toil of bills and debts...

1262
02:19:17,140 --> 02:19:21,400
...of mortgages and insurances, and all the evils attendant upon them.

1263
02:19:23,400 --> 02:19:27,150
Lady Lyndon's income was hampered almost irretrievably...

1264
02:19:27,610 --> 02:19:29,410
...to satisfy these claims.

1265
02:20:17,000 --> 02:20:18,370
Do you think that's good?

1266
02:20:18,540 --> 02:20:19,750
Excellent.

1267
02:20:20,330 --> 02:20:21,630
Who's this?

1268
02:20:22,000 --> 02:20:23,790
It's a peacock on the wall.

1269
02:20:25,800 --> 02:20:27,090
What's it say?

1270
02:20:27,460 --> 02:20:29,470
I saw this bird yesterday.

1271
02:20:34,720 --> 02:20:35,890
Who's that?

1272
02:20:36,560 --> 02:20:38,270
Mama in her coach.

1273
02:20:39,350 --> 02:20:40,980
Is she going to London?

1274
02:20:41,900 --> 02:20:43,060
I don't know.

1275
02:20:45,320 --> 02:20:46,400
Parry.

1276
02:20:46,570 --> 02:20:47,780
Parry.

1277
02:20:48,690 --> 02:20:49,740
Very good.

1278
02:20:49,900 --> 02:20:51,410
Barry had his faults...

1279
02:20:52,530 --> 02:20:56,620
...but no man could say of him that he was not a good and tender father.

1280
02:20:58,290 --> 02:21:00,910
He loved his son with a blind partiality.

1281
02:21:02,120 --> 02:21:03,670
He denied him nothing.

1282
02:21:05,340 --> 02:21:09,010
It is impossible to convey what high hopes he had for the boy...

1283
02:21:09,880 --> 02:21:13,180
...and how he indulged in a thousand fond anticipations...

1284
02:21:13,720 --> 02:21:16,680
...as to his future success and figure in the world.

1285
02:21:19,220 --> 02:21:23,520
But fate had determined that he should leave none of his race behind him...

1286
02:21:24,650 --> 02:21:26,730
...that he should finish his life...

1287
02:21:27,070 --> 02:21:29,070
...poor, lonely...

1288
02:21:29,690 --> 02:21:31,030
...and childless.

1289
02:21:40,000 --> 02:21:41,040
Papa.

1290
02:21:41,370 --> 02:21:42,460
Yes, Bryan?

1291
02:21:42,620 --> 02:21:44,420
Will you buy me a horse?

1292
02:21:44,580 --> 02:21:46,080
Buy you a horse?

1293
02:21:46,420 --> 02:21:47,540
Yes, Papa.

1294
02:21:48,090 --> 02:21:49,840
But you already have little Julia.

1295
02:21:50,010 --> 02:21:52,880
She's only a pony, I want a real horse.

1296
02:21:53,090 --> 02:21:55,260
Then I can ride with you on the hunt.

1297
02:21:55,720 --> 02:21:57,600
You think you're big enough for the hunt?

1298
02:21:57,760 --> 02:21:58,810
Oh, yes!

1299
02:21:58,970 --> 02:22:03,560
Jonathan Plunkett is only a year older than I am and he rides with his papa.

1300
02:22:04,770 --> 02:22:06,610
I'll have to think about it.

1301
02:22:06,900 --> 02:22:11,740
Please, say yes. There's nothing I want in the whole world more than a horse.

1302
02:22:13,070 --> 02:22:14,400
I'll think about it.

1303
02:22:14,570 --> 02:22:16,570
Oh, thank you, Papa. Thank you!

1304
02:22:21,290 --> 02:22:23,120
How much are you asking for him?

1305
02:22:23,750 --> 02:22:25,210
One hundred guineas.

1306
02:22:26,120 --> 02:22:29,460
He's a nice little horse, but I don't think he's worth that.

1307
02:22:30,130 --> 02:22:32,760
Seventy-five seems more like the right price.

1308
02:22:34,590 --> 02:22:37,640
I'll accept eighty guineas, and not a shilling less.

1309
02:22:39,600 --> 02:22:43,180
Five guineas should never keep two gentlemen from their drink. Eighty then.

1310
02:22:43,350 --> 02:22:44,390
Done, sir.

1311
02:22:45,640 --> 02:22:49,770
Take the horse to Doolan's farm. Tell him he needs a bit of breaking in.

1312
02:22:50,150 --> 02:22:53,780
Say it's for Master Bryan's birthday, and I want it to be a surprise.

1313
02:22:53,940 --> 02:22:55,240
And remember that yourself.

1314
02:22:55,400 --> 02:22:56,450
Yes, sir.

1315
02:23:41,120 --> 02:23:42,160
Papa.

1316
02:23:43,990 --> 02:23:45,450
What is it, lad?

1317
02:23:45,700 --> 02:23:47,330
Did you buy the horse?

1318
02:23:49,830 --> 02:23:51,540
What horse is that?

1319
02:23:52,290 --> 02:23:55,260
The horse you were going to buy me for my birthday.

1320
02:23:57,840 --> 02:23:59,800
I know nothing about any horse.

1321
02:24:00,550 --> 02:24:04,180
But one of the stable boys told Nelly you'd already bought it...

1322
02:24:04,810 --> 02:24:08,270
...and it was at Doolan's farm where Mick the groom was breaking it in.

1323
02:24:08,980 --> 02:24:10,230
Is that true?

1324
02:24:11,440 --> 02:24:13,020
When is your birthday?

1325
02:24:14,690 --> 02:24:15,860
Next Tuesday.

1326
02:24:18,030 --> 02:24:20,740
Well, you'll have to wait till then to find out.

1327
02:24:21,240 --> 02:24:23,870
Then, it's true! Oh, thank you, Papa.

1328
02:24:27,120 --> 02:24:28,330
Bryan?

1329
02:24:29,210 --> 02:24:30,250
Bryan?

1330
02:24:30,420 --> 02:24:31,500
Yes, Mama?

1331
02:24:32,630 --> 02:24:36,340
Promise me you won't ride that horse except with your father.

1332
02:24:37,760 --> 02:24:39,380
Yes, Mama, I promise.

1333
02:24:41,010 --> 02:24:43,220
And I promise you a good flogging...

1334
02:24:43,390 --> 02:24:46,970
...if you even go to Doolan's farm to see him before your birthday.

1335
02:24:47,560 --> 02:24:48,560
Yes, Papa.

1336
02:24:48,730 --> 02:24:49,730
You understand?

1337
02:24:49,890 --> 02:24:51,060
Yes, Papa.

1338
02:24:51,560 --> 02:24:52,560
You promise me?

1339
02:24:52,730 --> 02:24:54,230
Yes, Papa, I promise.

1340
02:24:56,400 --> 02:24:58,070
All right, eat your food.

1341
02:25:06,910 --> 02:25:08,160
Come in.

1342
02:25:21,800 --> 02:25:23,090
Good morning, sir.

1343
02:25:23,260 --> 02:25:24,430
Good morning, Reverend.

1344
02:25:24,680 --> 02:25:26,930
I'm sorry to trouble you, Mr. Lyndon...

1345
02:25:27,100 --> 02:25:29,430
...but I believe Master Bryan has disobeyed you...

1346
02:25:29,600 --> 02:25:31,770
...and stolen away to Doolan's farm.

1347
02:25:32,980 --> 02:25:36,270
On going to his room this morning, I found his bed empty.

1348
02:25:36,520 --> 02:25:39,440
One of the cooks saw him cross the yard at daybreak.

1349
02:25:39,610 --> 02:25:41,150
Didn't you see him go?

1350
02:25:41,320 --> 02:25:44,410
He must have passed through my room while I was asleep.

1351
02:26:16,810 --> 02:26:20,400
Oh, my God! What has happened here?

1352
02:26:20,610 --> 02:26:24,650
I noticed the lad riding across the field, sir...

1353
02:26:24,820 --> 02:26:28,200
...and having trouble with the horse, which was playing up.

1354
02:26:29,160 --> 02:26:33,330
Suddenly the animal plunged and reared, and the poor lad was thrown.

1355
02:26:36,540 --> 02:26:38,840
Oh, Bryan, why did you disobey me?

1356
02:26:40,550 --> 02:26:42,090
I'm sorry, Papa.

1357
02:26:42,880 --> 02:26:44,670
You won't whip me, will you?

1358
02:26:47,680 --> 02:26:49,100
No, my darling.

1359
02:26:50,260 --> 02:26:51,600
I won't whip you.

1360
02:26:55,890 --> 02:27:00,110
Take my horse and ride like the Devil for Doctor Broughton.

1361
02:27:00,940 --> 02:27:04,190
Tell him whatever he's doing he must come at once. Understand?

1362
02:27:04,570 --> 02:27:05,780
Yes, sir.

1363
02:27:14,290 --> 02:27:15,960
The doctors were called.

1364
02:27:17,000 --> 02:27:20,840
But what does a doctor avail in a contest with the grim, invincible enemy?

1365
02:27:22,630 --> 02:27:27,010
Such as came could only confirm the hopelessness of the poor child's case.

1366
02:27:29,180 --> 02:27:31,810
He remained with his parents for two days.

1367
02:27:33,180 --> 02:27:36,810
And a sad comfort it was to know he was in no pain.

1368
02:27:38,560 --> 02:27:39,730
Papa.

1369
02:27:47,820 --> 02:27:48,860
Papa.

1370
02:27:50,700 --> 02:27:52,580
Am I going to die?

1371
02:27:58,540 --> 02:28:02,920
No, my darling, you're not going to die. You're going to get better.

1372
02:28:07,220 --> 02:28:11,640
But I can't feel anything, except in my hands.

1373
02:28:14,600 --> 02:28:18,390
Does that mean I'm already dead in part of my body?

1374
02:28:26,150 --> 02:28:29,610
No, my darling, that's where you were hurt by the horse.

1375
02:28:32,320 --> 02:28:34,530
But you're going to be all right.

1376
02:28:39,290 --> 02:28:42,960
Papa, if I die, will I go to heaven?

1377
02:28:48,470 --> 02:28:50,510
Of course you will, my darling...

1378
02:28:52,180 --> 02:28:54,180
...but you're not going to die.

1379
02:28:59,100 --> 02:29:00,890
Mama, give me your hand.

1380
02:29:11,030 --> 02:29:12,820
Papa, give me your hand.

1381
02:29:19,120 --> 02:29:21,920
Will you both promise me something?

1382
02:29:25,170 --> 02:29:26,210
Yes.

1383
02:29:27,420 --> 02:29:30,670
Promise me never to quarrel so.

1384
02:29:31,260 --> 02:29:33,010
But to love each other.

1385
02:29:33,840 --> 02:29:37,760
So that we may meet again, in heaven...

1386
02:29:38,850 --> 02:29:43,230
...where Bullingdon said quarrelsome people will never go.

1387
02:29:51,610 --> 02:29:52,700
We promise.

1388
02:29:56,320 --> 02:29:59,410
Will you tell me the story about the fort?

1389
02:30:02,120 --> 02:30:03,210
Of course.

1390
02:30:07,380 --> 02:30:09,050
We crept up on the fort.

1391
02:30:12,880 --> 02:30:18,390
I jumped over the wall first, and my fellows jumped after me.

1392
02:30:19,560 --> 02:30:22,060
And you should have seen the look...

1393
02:30:23,690 --> 02:30:29,070
...on the Frenchmen's faces when 23 he-devils, sword and pistol...

1394
02:30:30,110 --> 02:30:33,570
...cut and thrust, came tumbling into the fort.

1395
02:30:35,070 --> 02:30:36,490
In three minutes...

1396
02:30:39,620 --> 02:30:40,830
...we left....

1397
02:30:51,210 --> 02:30:55,010
"'I am the resurrection and the life,' saith the Lord.

1398
02:30:56,430 --> 02:30:59,930
"'He that believeth in me, though he were dead...

1399
02:31:01,600 --> 02:31:03,220
"'...yet shall he live.

1400
02:31:04,230 --> 02:31:07,810
"'And whosoever liveth and believeth in me...

1401
02:31:09,230 --> 02:31:10,730
"'...shall never die.'

1402
02:31:13,530 --> 02:31:15,990
"I know that my Redeemer liveth...

1403
02:31:16,400 --> 02:31:19,870
"...and that He shall stand at the latter day upon the earth.

1404
02:31:20,910 --> 02:31:24,700
"And though after my skin worms destroy this body...

1405
02:31:25,410 --> 02:31:28,830
"...yet in my flesh shall I see God.

1406
02:31:30,630 --> 02:31:34,840
"Whom I shall see for myself and mine eyes shall behold...

1407
02:31:35,720 --> 02:31:37,220
"...and not another.

1408
02:31:39,680 --> 02:31:41,930
"We brought nothing into this world...

1409
02:31:42,510 --> 02:31:45,350
"...and it is certain we can carry nothing out.

1410
02:31:46,640 --> 02:31:51,610
"The Lord gave, and the Lord hath taken away.

1411
02:31:52,980 --> 02:31:55,320
"Blessed is the name of the Lord."

1412
02:32:40,200 --> 02:32:42,490
Barry's grief was inconsolable.

1413
02:32:43,950 --> 02:32:47,830
Such solace as he could find, came only from drink.

1414
02:32:50,790 --> 02:32:56,000
His mother was the only person who would remain faithful to him in his misfortune.

1415
02:32:56,920 --> 02:33:00,550
And many a night, when he was unconscious of her attention...

1416
02:33:00,970 --> 02:33:02,930
...saw him carried off to bed.

1417
02:33:08,560 --> 02:33:09,930
"O blessed Lord...

1418
02:33:10,390 --> 02:33:13,350
"...the Father of mercies and God of all comforts.

1419
02:33:13,940 --> 02:33:17,570
"We beseech Thee, look down in pity and compassion upon this...

1420
02:33:17,730 --> 02:33:19,440
"...Thy afflicted servant.

1421
02:33:20,650 --> 02:33:22,660
"Thou writest bitter things against her...

1422
02:33:22,820 --> 02:33:25,240
"...and makest her to possess her former iniquities."

1423
02:33:25,410 --> 02:33:28,620
Her Ladyship, always vaporish and nervous...

1424
02:33:29,450 --> 02:33:31,160
...plunged into devotion...

1425
02:33:31,330 --> 02:33:36,290
...with so much fervor that you would have imagined her distracted at times.

1426
02:34:01,320 --> 02:34:05,280
In the doleful conditions which now prevailed at Castle Hackton...

1427
02:34:06,070 --> 02:34:09,620
...management of the household, and of the Lyndon estate...

1428
02:34:10,290 --> 02:34:13,460
...fell to Mrs. Barry, whose spirit of order...

1429
02:34:13,710 --> 02:34:18,130
...attended to all the details of a great establishment.

1430
02:34:22,300 --> 02:34:23,470
Come in.

1431
02:34:28,050 --> 02:34:29,810
You wish to see me, madam?

1432
02:34:30,310 --> 02:34:32,640
Yes, Reverend. Please sit down.

1433
02:34:37,440 --> 02:34:40,320
I have some matters I would like to discuss later, Graham...

1434
02:34:40,480 --> 02:34:42,650
...but just now would you go to Her Ladyship...

1435
02:34:42,820 --> 02:34:44,610
...and have her sign these papers.

1436
02:34:45,610 --> 02:34:46,740
Yes, madam.

1437
02:34:59,540 --> 02:35:01,000
Reverend Runt...

1438
02:35:02,130 --> 02:35:05,550
...I need not tell you that the recent tragedy to this family...

1439
02:35:05,720 --> 02:35:10,010
...has made the services of a tutor no longer required here.

1440
02:35:11,560 --> 02:35:14,850
And as we are in considerable difficulty about money...

1441
02:35:15,440 --> 02:35:19,060
...I'm afraid I must ask you, with the greatest reluctance...

1442
02:35:19,980 --> 02:35:21,610
...to resign your post.

1443
02:35:27,610 --> 02:35:31,030
Madam, I'm sensible of your predicament...

1444
02:35:31,870 --> 02:35:36,750
...and you need have no concern about my wages, I can willingly forego them...

1445
02:35:37,670 --> 02:35:42,130
...but it is out of the question for me to leave Her Ladyship in her present state.

1446
02:35:45,720 --> 02:35:47,800
I'm sorry to say this to you...

1447
02:35:48,590 --> 02:35:53,850
...but I believe you are largely responsible for her present state of mind.

1448
02:35:54,890 --> 02:35:58,100
The sooner you leave, the better she will be.

1449
02:36:03,270 --> 02:36:05,570
Madam, with the greatest respect...

1450
02:36:06,150 --> 02:36:08,950
...I take my instructions only from Her Ladyship.

1451
02:36:11,740 --> 02:36:13,120
Reverend Runt...

1452
02:36:13,830 --> 02:36:17,790
...Her Ladyship is in no fit mind to give instructions to anyone.

1453
02:36:19,460 --> 02:36:22,960
My son has charged me with managing the affairs at Castle Hackton...

1454
02:36:23,130 --> 02:36:27,420
...until he recovers from his grief and resumes his interest in worldly matters.

1455
02:36:28,420 --> 02:36:30,590
And while I'm in charge...

1456
02:36:30,760 --> 02:36:33,220
...you'll take your instructions from me.

1457
02:36:33,850 --> 02:36:36,020
My only concern is for Lady Lyndon.

1458
02:36:39,310 --> 02:36:40,350
Madam...

1459
02:36:42,150 --> 02:36:45,360
...your only concern is for Her Ladyship's signature.

1460
02:36:46,320 --> 02:36:51,280
You and your son have almost succeeded in destroying a fine family fortune.

1461
02:36:52,160 --> 02:36:53,950
And what little remains for you...

1462
02:36:54,120 --> 02:36:57,290
...depends on keeping Her Ladyship prisoner in her own home.

1463
02:36:57,700 --> 02:36:59,160
Reverend Runt...

1464
02:37:00,160 --> 02:37:03,170
...this matter bears no further discussion.

1465
02:37:03,630 --> 02:37:06,250
You will pack your bags and leave by tomorrow.

1466
02:37:26,110 --> 02:37:28,900
God, help. Help!

1467
02:37:32,240 --> 02:37:34,870
In the midst of these great perplexities...

1468
02:37:35,030 --> 02:37:38,660
...Her Ladyship made an attempt to kill herself by taking poison.

1469
02:37:39,870 --> 02:37:42,710
Though she only made herself dangerously ill...

1470
02:37:43,580 --> 02:37:46,340
...due to the very small amount which she swallowed...

1471
02:37:46,500 --> 02:37:50,630
...this, nevertheless, caused an intervention from a certain quarter...

1472
02:37:51,050 --> 02:37:52,630
...which was long overdue.

1473
02:37:52,800 --> 02:37:54,010
Oh, my God!

1474
02:38:26,540 --> 02:38:28,210
If my mother had died...

1475
02:38:29,250 --> 02:38:31,550
...it would've been as much my responsibility...

1476
02:38:31,710 --> 02:38:34,510
...as if I had poured the strychnine for her myself.

1477
02:38:36,220 --> 02:38:39,220
For to the everlasting disgrace of my family name...

1478
02:38:40,310 --> 02:38:43,560
...I have, by my cowardice and my weakness...

1479
02:38:44,640 --> 02:38:47,400
...allowed the Barrys to establish a brutal...

1480
02:38:47,730 --> 02:38:50,190
...and ignorant tyranny over our lives...

1481
02:38:50,690 --> 02:38:53,150
...which has left my mother a broken woman...

1482
02:38:54,240 --> 02:38:59,240
...and to squander and ruin a fine family fortune.

1483
02:39:02,250 --> 02:39:06,580
My friends profess sympathy, but behind my back...

1484
02:39:07,460 --> 02:39:09,090
...I know I am despised.

1485
02:39:10,590 --> 02:39:12,460
And quite justifiably so.

1486
02:39:19,890 --> 02:39:20,970
However...

1487
02:39:24,890 --> 02:39:26,770
...I know now what I must do.

1488
02:39:29,770 --> 02:39:31,230
And what I shall do.

1489
02:39:33,690 --> 02:39:35,190
Whatever be the cost.

1490
02:39:49,420 --> 02:39:51,130
Good morning, My Lord.

1491
02:39:51,540 --> 02:39:52,750
Good morning.

1492
02:39:53,750 --> 02:39:55,210
Is Mr. Barry Lyndon here?

1493
02:39:55,380 --> 02:39:57,130
Yes, My Lord, he's inside.

1494
02:39:57,840 --> 02:39:59,050
Thank you.

1495
02:41:35,980 --> 02:41:37,400
Mr. Redmond Barry.

1496
02:41:41,240 --> 02:41:43,490
The last occasion on which we met...

1497
02:41:44,280 --> 02:41:47,160
...you wantonly caused me injury and dishonor.

1498
02:41:48,740 --> 02:41:53,670
In such a manner, and to such an extent no gentleman can willingly suffer...

1499
02:41:55,380 --> 02:41:57,590
...without demanding satisfaction...

1500
02:41:58,670 --> 02:42:00,670
...however much time intervenes.

1501
02:42:05,010 --> 02:42:08,390
I have now come to claim that satisfaction.

1502
02:42:53,310 --> 02:42:54,440
Mr. Lyndon...

1503
02:42:54,890 --> 02:42:57,810
...these are a matched pair of pistols, and as you have seen...

1504
02:42:57,980 --> 02:43:01,320
...your second has loaded one, and I have loaded the other.

1505
02:43:01,690 --> 02:43:05,990
As they belong to Lord Bullingdon, you may have whichever one you wish.

1506
02:43:14,040 --> 02:43:15,500
Lord Bullingdon.

1507
02:43:23,510 --> 02:43:27,220
Now, gentlemen, to determine who will have first fire...

1508
02:43:27,760 --> 02:43:29,680
...I will toss a coin in the air.

1509
02:43:29,890 --> 02:43:33,850
As the offended party, it is Lord Bullingdon's choice to call the toss.

1510
02:43:35,020 --> 02:43:37,270
Is that agreeable to both of you?

1511
02:43:39,650 --> 02:43:40,650
Yes.

1512
02:43:43,030 --> 02:43:46,860
If Lord Bullingdon calls correctly he will have the first fire.

1513
02:43:48,240 --> 02:43:52,080
If incorrectly, Mr. Lyndon will have the first fire.

1514
02:43:53,910 --> 02:43:55,750
Is that clearly understood?

1515
02:44:03,590 --> 02:44:05,920
What is your call, Lord Bullingdon?

1516
02:44:07,470 --> 02:44:08,510
Heads.

1517
02:44:17,230 --> 02:44:18,390
It is heads.

1518
02:44:20,060 --> 02:44:22,520
Lord Bullingdon will have the first fire.

1519
02:44:26,940 --> 02:44:28,450
Lord Bullingdon...

1520
02:44:28,990 --> 02:44:30,950
...will you take your ground?

1521
02:44:44,800 --> 02:44:47,130
One...two...three...

1522
02:44:47,300 --> 02:44:49,130
...four...five...six...

1523
02:44:49,430 --> 02:44:52,600
...seven...eight...nine...ten.

1524
02:44:58,810 --> 02:45:01,770
Mr. Lyndon, will you take your ground?

1525
02:45:23,000 --> 02:45:27,300
Mr. Lyndon, are you ready to receive Lord Bullingdon's fire?

1526
02:45:38,850 --> 02:45:39,930
Yes.

1527
02:45:43,730 --> 02:45:45,110
Lord Bullingdon...

1528
02:45:46,480 --> 02:45:48,150
...cock your pistol...

1529
02:45:48,780 --> 02:45:50,530
...and prepare to fire.

1530
02:46:05,500 --> 02:46:07,880
Sir Richard, this pistol must be faulty.

1531
02:46:08,840 --> 02:46:10,630
I must have another one.

1532
02:46:11,590 --> 02:46:14,970
I'm sorry, Lord Bullingdon, but you must first stand your ground...

1533
02:46:15,140 --> 02:46:17,550
...and allow Mr. Lyndon his turn to fire.

1534
02:46:20,890 --> 02:46:23,020
That is correct, Lord Bullingdon.

1535
02:46:23,850 --> 02:46:27,900
Your pistol has fired, and that counts as your shot.

1536
02:46:42,290 --> 02:46:46,250
Mr. Lyndon, are the rules of firing clear to you?

1537
02:46:48,210 --> 02:46:49,250
Yes.

1538
02:46:53,130 --> 02:46:54,550
Lord Bullingdon...

1539
02:46:56,050 --> 02:46:58,680
...are you ready to receive Mr. Lyndon's fire?

1540
02:47:05,560 --> 02:47:06,600
Yes.

1541
02:47:09,860 --> 02:47:11,320
Very well, then.

1542
02:47:14,740 --> 02:47:16,070
Mr. Lyndon...

1543
02:47:17,410 --> 02:47:18,990
...cock your pistol...

1544
02:47:19,950 --> 02:47:21,580
...and prepare to fire.

1545
02:48:18,630 --> 02:48:21,640
Are you ready, Lord Bullingdon?

1546
02:48:31,150 --> 02:48:33,360
Is your pistol cocked, Mr. Lyndon?

1547
02:48:36,490 --> 02:48:37,530
Yes.

1548
02:48:42,740 --> 02:48:44,240
Then prepare to fire.

1549
02:48:50,920 --> 02:48:52,040
One...

1550
02:48:53,790 --> 02:48:54,920
...two....

1551
02:49:07,350 --> 02:49:11,690
Lord Bullingdon, in view of Mr. Lyndon having fired into the ground...

1552
02:49:12,190 --> 02:49:15,440
...do you now consider that you have received satisfaction?

1553
02:49:29,250 --> 02:49:32,120
I have not received satisfaction.

1554
02:50:03,950 --> 02:50:06,200
Mr. Lyndon, are you ready?

1555
02:50:17,590 --> 02:50:18,630
Yes.

1556
02:50:21,170 --> 02:50:22,550
Lord Bullingdon...

1557
02:50:23,510 --> 02:50:25,970
...cock your pistol and get ready to fire.

1558
02:50:44,570 --> 02:50:45,700
One...

1559
02:50:48,450 --> 02:50:49,620
...two....

1560
02:51:09,810 --> 02:51:14,060
Barry was carried to an inn nearby and a surgeon was called.

1561
02:51:28,200 --> 02:51:29,660
I'm nearly finished.

1562
02:51:51,850 --> 02:51:54,850
I'm sorry to have to tell you this, Mr. Lyndon.

1563
02:51:55,980 --> 02:51:58,350
I'm afraid you'll have to lose the leg...

1564
02:51:59,190 --> 02:52:01,020
...most likely below the knee.

1565
02:52:09,950 --> 02:52:11,240
Lose the leg?

1566
02:52:12,950 --> 02:52:14,120
What for?

1567
02:52:16,200 --> 02:52:18,710
The simple answer is to save your life.

1568
02:52:20,130 --> 02:52:24,250
The ball has shattered the bone below the knee and severed the artery.

1569
02:52:25,590 --> 02:52:28,680
Unless I amputate, there's no way that I can repair the artery...

1570
02:52:28,840 --> 02:52:30,550
...and stop the hemorrhage.

1571
02:53:02,420 --> 02:53:03,460
Graham.

1572
02:53:03,670 --> 02:53:04,920
Yes, My Lord?

1573
02:53:05,300 --> 02:53:09,930
When we arrive at Castle Hackton, inform Mrs. Barry of what has happened.

1574
02:53:10,840 --> 02:53:13,100
Don't go into any unnecessary detail.

1575
02:53:13,430 --> 02:53:17,350
Just tell her where he is and that he has been wounded in the leg.

1576
02:53:18,180 --> 02:53:19,980
She will want to go to him.

1577
02:53:20,600 --> 02:53:24,730
See that she is out of the house and on her way to London as soon as possible.

1578
02:53:24,900 --> 02:53:25,900
And in no event...

1579
02:53:26,070 --> 02:53:28,820
...is she to be allowed to see my mother...

1580
02:53:29,070 --> 02:53:32,360
...or create any disturbance at the house before she leaves.

1581
02:53:33,120 --> 02:53:34,320
Yes, My Lord.

1582
02:54:46,900 --> 02:54:48,860
Ah, Mrs. Barry, how do you do?

1583
02:54:49,860 --> 02:54:51,940
How nice to see you, Graham. Come in.

1584
02:54:52,110 --> 02:54:53,150
Thank you.

1585
02:54:54,820 --> 02:54:57,700
You, uh...You received my note?

1586
02:54:58,030 --> 02:54:59,530
Yes, we were expecting you.

1587
02:54:59,700 --> 02:55:02,410
Oh, good, I didn't want to call unannounced.

1588
02:55:03,710 --> 02:55:05,710
Mr. Lyndon, how are you feeling?

1589
02:55:07,710 --> 02:55:10,380
I'm feeling much better, thank you, Graham.

1590
02:55:11,710 --> 02:55:12,920
Won't you sit down?

1591
02:55:13,090 --> 02:55:14,630
Thank you, Mrs. Barry.

1592
02:55:23,180 --> 02:55:24,810
Would you like some tea?

1593
02:55:24,980 --> 02:55:28,150
Oh, no! No, thank you, Mrs. Barry. Not just now.

1594
02:55:33,900 --> 02:55:35,990
How's the world treating you, Graham?

1595
02:55:36,150 --> 02:55:37,740
Oh, not too bad.

1596
02:55:40,910 --> 02:55:42,870
Are you comfortable here?

1597
02:55:43,040 --> 02:55:44,330
Most comfortable.

1598
02:55:44,500 --> 02:55:45,750
Good, good!

1599
02:55:53,920 --> 02:55:55,300
Well, uh...

1600
02:55:56,420 --> 02:55:57,590
...shall, uh, ahem....

1601
02:56:00,100 --> 02:56:02,680
Shall we get down to the matter at hand?

1602
02:56:04,640 --> 02:56:05,850
By all means.

1603
02:56:06,560 --> 02:56:07,690
Yes.

1604
02:56:09,520 --> 02:56:10,770
Well....

1605
02:56:12,480 --> 02:56:13,650
Mr. Lyndon...

1606
02:56:16,650 --> 02:56:19,490
...Lord Bullingdon has instructed me...

1607
02:56:20,320 --> 02:56:23,790
...to offer you an annuity...

1608
02:56:24,290 --> 02:56:28,330
...of 500 guineas a year for life.

1609
02:56:30,500 --> 02:56:33,630
Specifically on the condition of your...

1610
02:56:35,630 --> 02:56:37,220
...leaving England...

1611
02:56:37,840 --> 02:56:40,510
...and to be stopped...

1612
02:56:41,640 --> 02:56:43,850
...the instant of your return.

1613
02:56:46,930 --> 02:56:51,270
Lord Bullingdon has also asked me to point out to you...

1614
02:56:52,690 --> 02:56:55,320
...that should you decide to remain here...

1615
02:56:56,030 --> 02:56:59,780
...your stay would infallibly plunge you...

1616
02:57:01,490 --> 02:57:03,370
...into jail.

1617
02:57:05,040 --> 02:57:09,710
As in view of the present circumstances there will soon be...

1618
02:57:10,710 --> 02:57:13,880
...innumerable writs taken out against you for...

1619
02:57:14,340 --> 02:57:16,460
...debts long-outstanding...

1620
02:57:17,710 --> 02:57:20,380
...and your credit is so blown that...

1621
02:57:21,890 --> 02:57:23,550
...you could not hope...

1622
02:57:24,180 --> 02:57:25,810
...to raise a shilling.

1623
02:57:56,460 --> 02:57:58,590
Utterly baffled and beaten...

1624
02:58:00,420 --> 02:58:03,390
...what was the lonely and broken-hearted man to do?

1625
02:58:05,350 --> 02:58:09,100
He took the annuity and returned to Ireland with his mother...

1626
02:58:09,770 --> 02:58:11,430
...to complete his recovery.

1627
02:58:12,940 --> 02:58:15,560
Sometime later he traveled abroad.

1628
02:58:17,570 --> 02:58:21,360
His life there, we have not the means of following accurately.

1629
02:58:22,280 --> 02:58:25,780
He appears to have resumed his former profession of a gambler...

1630
02:58:25,950 --> 02:58:27,910
...without his former success.

1631
02:58:29,410 --> 02:58:31,450
He never saw Lady Lyndon again.

